/* ================= CBLE · Customs Broker License Examination (U.S. Customs and Border Protection) =================
   Questions: the real CBP exams of April 2026 and October 2026 with CBP's official answer keys (U.S. government works).
   Practical-exercise questions that need the exam's attached forms are left out. Explanations point to CBP's own citations.
   Areas: 1 Broker compliance (19 CFR 111) · 2 Entry and entry summary · 3 Classification (HTSUS) · 4 Valuation, appraisement and duty · 5 Quota. */
F(9,1,"Who can get a broker license","U.S. citizen, at least 21, not an officer or employee of the U.S. government, of good moral character, and has passed the CBLE (19 CFR 111.11).");
F(9,1,"Broker records","A broker keeps records of its customs business for 5 years from the date of entry, unless a shorter period applies (19 CFR 111.23).");
F(9,1,"Continuing broker education","An individual broker needs 36 credits of qualifying continuing education in each triennial reporting period (19 CFR 111.103).");
F(9,1,"Triennial status report","Every license holder files a status report every three years confirming it still meets the license and permit requirements (19 CFR 111.30(d)).");
F(9,1,"Customs power of attorney","CBP Form 5291 is the customs power of attorney; a broker must hold a valid power of attorney from the client before transacting its customs business (19 CFR 141.32, 141.46).");
F(9,1,"Responsible supervision and control","A broker must exercise responsible supervision and control over the customs business it conducts, including its employees (19 CFR 111.1, 111.28).");
F(9,2,"When to file entry","Entry documentation is generally due within 15 calendar days after the goods arrive at the port (19 CFR 142.2).");
F(9,2,"Entry summary deadline","The entry summary with estimated duties is due within 10 working days after the goods are entered or released (19 CFR 142.12(b)).");
F(9,2,"Informal entry limit","Most commercial shipments valued at $2,500 or less may use an informal entry (19 CFR 143.21).");
F(9,2,"Common entry type codes","01 consumption, 06 consumption from a foreign-trade zone, 11 informal, 21 warehouse (ACE Entry Summary Instructions).");
F(9,2,"Liquidation","An entry is normally liquidated within 1 year from the date of entry; CBP may extend, but not past 4 years total (19 CFR 159.11, 159.12).");
F(9,2,"Protest deadline","A protest is filed within 180 days after the date of liquidation or the decision protested (19 CFR 174.12).");
F(9,2,"User fees","Merchandise processing fee 0.3464% of value, with a yearly minimum and maximum; harbor maintenance fee 0.125% for goods arriving by vessel at covered ports (19 CFR 24.23, 24.24).");
F(9,3,"GRI 1","Classification is decided first by the terms of the headings and any section or chapter notes. The General Rules of Interpretation are applied in order.");
F(9,3,"GRI 3","When goods fit two headings: (a) the most specific description wins; (b) mixtures and sets go by essential character; (c) otherwise the heading that comes last in number order.");
F(9,3,"The 10-digit number","The first 6 digits are the international Harmonized System; 8 digits set the U.S. duty rate; digits 9-10 are the statistical suffix.");
F(9,3,"9802.00.80","Goods assembled abroad from U.S.-made parts that were only assembled: duty is charged on the full value minus the value of the U.S. components.");
F(9,3,"Chapters 98 and 99","Chapter 98 holds special classification provisions (returns, assemblies, personal exemptions); Chapter 99 holds temporary and additional duties.");
F(9,4,"Valuation order","Transaction value first, then identical merchandise, similar merchandise, deductive value, computed value, and last the fallback method (19 CFR 152.101). The importer may ask to use computed before deductive.");
F(9,4,"Transaction value","The price actually paid or payable for the goods when sold for export to the U.S., plus packing, selling commissions, assists, royalties and proceeds paid by the buyer (19 CFR 152.103).");
F(9,4,"Assists","Items or services supplied free or at reduced cost by the buyer for producing the goods, such as materials, tools, molds and foreign engineering work; their value is added to transaction value (19 CFR 152.102(a)).");
F(9,4,"What is not in value","International freight and insurance to the U.S., and U.S. duties and taxes, are not part of the dutiable value.");
F(9,5,"Tariff-rate quota","A set quantity enters at a lower duty rate; imports above that quantity still enter but pay a higher rate.");
F(9,5,"Absolute quota","Once the quantity is filled, no more of the goods may be entered for consumption until the next quota period.");
Q(9,1,2,"On January 31, 2021, ABC Imports (ABC) issued a power of attorney to XYZ Brokers, LLC (XYZ) granting XYZ the authority to transact customs business on behalf of ABC. Of the four choices below, which scenario describes a conflict of interest?","An employee of XYZ imports merchandise of the same general character as ABC without ABC’s knowledge.",["An employee of XYZ has a personal friendship with an employee of ABC.", "XYZ has several clients that import merchandise of the same general character as ABC.", "XYZ imports merchandise of the same general character as its client after providing the client full knowledge of the facts."],"Official answer: An employee of XYZ imports merchandise of the same general character as ABC without ABC’s knowledge. Look it up: 19 CFR 111.31. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 1.)");
Q(9,1,2,"Of the four choices below, who is responsible for including a statement in their triennial status report asserting whether he, she, or it still meets the applicable requirements of 19 CFR 111.11 and 111.19 to qualify for a customs broker license and national permit?","A corporation with an active customs broker license and national permit.",["A freight forwarder with a filer code authorized to file Importer Security Filing (ISF) data with CBP.", "An individual broker whose license was revoked by operation of law during the reporting period.", "A self-filing importer who has a filer code but not a customs broker license."],"Official answer: A corporation with an active customs broker license and national permit. Look it up: 19 CFR 111.30(d)(3)(i). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 2.)");
Q(9,1,2,"Licensed customs broker Dennis Parker was the corporate license qualifier and sole qualifying officer for Owl B There Logistics (Owl B) until his employment was terminated six months ago. Mr. Parker immediately provided written notice to CBP of his termination, but Owl B has not yet identified a new qualifying officer. Over the past six months, Owl B has continued to transact customs business on behalf of clients. Based on this scenario, which ONE of the following statements is FALSE?","Owl B is liable for a monetary penalty for each transaction of customs business conducted after Mr. Parker’s termination, not to exceed an aggregate of $10,000.00 for all transactions.",["Owl B has demonstrated grounds for the suspension or revocation of its corporate license under 19 CFR 111.53(h). CBP may revoke Owl B's license and national permit by operation of law.", "Mr. Parker fulfilled his responsibility to CBP as an individually licensed broker and is not liable for violations made by Owl B after his termination.", "In addition to transacting customs business without a qualifying officer, Owl B also failed to provide CBP with timely notification of a change in its organization."],"Official answer: Owl B is liable for a monetary penalty for each transaction of customs business conducted after Mr. Parker’s termination, not to exceed an aggregate of $10,000.00 for all transactions. Look it up: 19 CFR 111.4; 19 CFR 111.91(b). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 3.)");
Q(9,1,3,"A corporate customs broker, Global Trade Solutions Inc. (Global), employs multiple licensed customs brokers (LCBs), some of whom are corporate officers and some of whom are employees. Global's responsible supervision and control plan designates a specific LCB as the broker of record for each client account. One of Global’s LCB employees, Julia Pryne, has been on extended leave for several months. During this time, an unlicensed employee who normally works under Ms. Pryne’s supervision has been signing customs entry documents and communicating directly with CBP on behalf of Ms. Pryne’s client, without Ms. Pryne’s knowledge or guidance. Global’s corporate officers and other LCB employees are aware of the situation but do not intervene. If CBP determines this scenario constitutes a breach of responsible supervision and control, who is accountable for the breach?","Every licensed officer of Global Trade Solutions Inc.",["Julia Pryne", "Julia Pryne’s unlicensed employee", "All licensed officers and licensed employees of Global Trade Solutions Inc."],"Official answer: Every licensed officer of Global Trade Solutions Inc. Look it up: 19 CFR 111.1; 19 CFR 111.28(a). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 4.)");
Q(9,1,2,"Which ONE of the following individuals is required to complete 36 credits of qualifying continuing broker education within the 2027-2030 triennial reporting period?","An individually licensed customs broker who has continuously held his license since 2026.",["An individually licensed customs broker who has held his license since 2005 and voluntarily suspended his license from January 2025 through December 2035.", "An individually licensed customs broker who has held her license since 2024, voluntarily suspended her license in June 2026, and reinstated her license in January 2029.", "An individually licensed customs broker who has continuously held her license since 2028."],"Official answer: An individually licensed customs broker who has continuously held his license since 2026. Look it up: 19 CFR 111.102(a). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 5.)");
Q(9,1,2,"Beginning in 2027, when must an individually licensed customs broker subject to the continuing broker education requirement report and certify completion of their continuing education credits?","Triennially, or every three years",["At the end of each fiscal year", "Upon completion of the required number of qualifying continuing broker education credits", "Only upon receiving a request from CBP"],"Official answer: Triennially, or every three years Look it up: 19 CFR 111.102(c). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 6.)");
Q(9,1,2,"Which ONE of the following statements regarding the accreditation and providers of qualifying continuing broker education is TRUE?","Any training or educational activity whose content is relevant to customs business offered by CBP does not require accreditation.",["A CBP-selected accreditor must review and accredit training offered by a U.S. government agency for the training to be qualified for continuing broker education credit.", "Qualifying continuing broker education may only be offered by private entities that have successfully passed the CBP-selected accreditor evaluation process.", "Educational activities offered by another U.S. government agency are strictly excluded from qualifying as continuing broker education."],"Official answer: Any training or educational activity whose content is relevant to customs business offered by CBP does not require accreditation. Look it up: 19 CFR 111.103. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 7.)");
Q(9,1,2,"CBP issued a notice to individually licensed customs broker Ivette Perez, indicating that Ms. Perez had failed to record and certify compliance with the continuing broker education requirement in accordance with 19 CFR 111.30(d). To prevent her license from being suspended, Ms. Perez must submit a corrected status report, reflecting her compliance with the continuing broker education requirement on or before _____ from the issuance of CBP’s notification.","30 calendar days",["15 business days", "60 calendar days", "60 business days"],"Official answer: 30 calendar days Look it up: 19 CFR 111.104(b)(2). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 8.)");
Q(9,1,3,"John Eastman earned his broker license in 1980 and was admitted to the Court of International Trade in 1990. He is retired and is not actively engaged in transacting business as a broker. As an expert in Customs law, he presented at WESTCON 2025, an annual convention of customs brokers and freight forwarders. WESTCON is an educational activity approved for continuing education credit by a CBP-selected accreditor, and Mr. Eastman was approved as a speaker at WESTCON. Mr. Eastman spent three hours preparing a presentation on Section 301 of the Trade Act of 1974 and two hours delivering it. Based on this scenario, what is the maximum number of continuing education credits that Mr. Eastman may claim for his WESTCON 2025 presentation?","2",["0", "3", "5"],"Official answer: 2 Look it up: 19 CFR 111.102; 19 CFR 111.103; 19 CFR 111.30(d). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 9.)");
Q(9,1,2,"Which ONE of the following actions is NOT grounds for the suspension or revocation of a broker's license?","A broker knowingly works with a client whose broker license was cancelled “with prejudice.”",["A broker knowingly made a misleading statement on his or her license application.", "A broker was convicted of a felony involving embezzlement.", "A broker knowingly employed a felon without CBP approval."],"Official answer: A broker knowingly works with a client whose broker license was cancelled “with prejudice.” Look it up: 19 CFR 111.53. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 10.)");
Q(9,1,3,"Ace Brokerage, Inc. (Ace Brokerage) is a wholly-owned subsidiary of Ace Logistics, Inc. (Ace Logistics) and Ace Brokerage holds a customs broker license and national permit. The power of attorney agreements between Ace Brokerage and its clients contain no language making the agreement binding on Ace Brokerage’s heirs, assigns, or successors. In 2026, Ace Brokerage will merge into Ace Logistics, and Ace Brokerage will cease to exist. Prior to the merger, Ace Logistics is not a licensed customs broker. Ace Logistics intends to conduct customs business on behalf of Ace Brokerage’s former clients following the merger. Based on this scenario, which ONE of the following statements is TRUE?","Ace Logistics must obtain new powers of attorney before it can transact Customs business on behalf of Ace Brokerage’s former clients.",["Ace Logistics must designate a licensed officer within 120 days of the merger in order to maintain use of Ace Brokerage’s customs broker license.", "Ace Brokerage’s national permit is transferable to Ace Logistics.", "Ace Logistics may transact customs business under Ace Brokerage’s license once the merger is complete."],"Official answer: Ace Logistics must obtain new powers of attorney before it can transact Customs business on behalf of Ace Brokerage’s former clients. Look it up: 19 CFR 141.32; 19 CFR 141.37; 19 CFR 141.46. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 11.)");
Q(9,1,2,"Which ONE of the following business relationships is prohibited for customs brokers?","Compensating an unlicensed importer for referring brokerage business.",["Compensating an unlicensed freight forwarder for the client’s freight charges.", "Charging higher than market rate for services rendered to importers.", "Charging a duty outlay fee for processing duty payments for importers."],"Official answer: Compensating an unlicensed importer for referring brokerage business. Look it up: 19 CFR 111.36(b)-(c). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 12.)");
Q(9,1,3,"Kristine Cheng received her individual customs broker license in September 2025. In November 2025, Ms. Cheng received her national permit and filer code and began conducting customs business from her office in Los Angeles, California. In December 2025, she hired Michael Yu, and in January 2026, she hired John Xiao. Neither Mr. Yu nor Mr. Xiao was licensed as a customs broker, but they both prepared, entered, and transmitted entries on behalf of Ms. Cheng. Ms. Cheng executed a power of attorney that authorized Mr. Yu to sign customs documents, but Ms. Cheng did not file the power of attorney with CBP. She did not execute a customs power of attorney with Mr. Xiao but filed a statement with the processing Center that identified him as an employee authorized to transact customs business on her behalf. In April 2026, Mr. Yu moved to Toronto, Canada. He worked remotely for Ms. Cheng until she found a new employee in May 2026. Upon Mr. Yu's departure from the business, Ms. Cheng promptly notified the processing Center of the change in employee information. Of the four choices below, which ONE of Ms. Cheng’s actions violated CBP regulations?","Ms. Cheng allowed Mr. Yu to work remotely in Toronto, Canada.",["Ms. Cheng allowed unlicensed employees to conduct customs business on her behalf.", "Ms. Cheng failed to file Mr. Yu’s power of attorney with CBP.", "Ms. Cheng allowed Mr. Xiao to work for her without having executed a power of attorney that authorized him to conduct customs business on her behalf."],"Official answer: Ms. Cheng allowed Mr. Yu to work remotely in Toronto, Canada. Look it up: 19 CFR 111.2(a)(2)(ii); 19 CFR 111.3(a). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 13.)");
Q(9,1,2,"Customs Broker Jones receives an electronic notification via ABI Message UC2 from CBP requesting documents for an entry submitted that same day. How must Broker Jones respond?","Produce the records by the time of entry summary filing.",["Contact CBP to request that an official CBP Form 28, Request for Information, be issued specifying the documents requested.", "Produce the records within 30 calendar days of the receipt of demand.", "Wait for CBP to specify the due date before producing the records."],"Official answer: Produce the records by the time of entry summary filing. Look it up: 19 CFR 142.3; ACE BRPD Section 1.13. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 14.)");
Q(9,1,2,"Upon the permanent termination of an individual broker’s brokerage business, what information must the broker include in his written notification to the processing Center?","The name, address, email address, and telephone number of the party with legal custody of the brokerage business records",["The names and contact information of the business's clients", "A final triennial status report covering all of the brokerage business conducted from the last status report through the date of termination", "Records from the terminated business for all customs transactions over the previous six months"],"Official answer: The name, address, email address, and telephone number of the party with legal custody of the brokerage business records Look it up: 19 CFR 111.30(e). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 15.)");
Q(9,1,2,"CBP provided XYZ Brokers with a written demand to produce entry records related to the eligibility of unliquidated entries for a column 1 special rate of duty in the Harmonized Tariff Schedule (HTSUS). If XYZ Brokers fails to timely produce the requested records as a result of negligence, which penalty may CBP impose, absent any exceptions?","Liquidate the subject entries at the applicable HTSUS column 1 general rate of duty, in addition to the applicable monetary penalty.",["The subject merchandise shall be considered abandoned and may be put up for sale at public auction.", "Suspend XYZ Broker’s national permit until XYZ Brokers can produce the requested records, in addition to the applicable monetary penalty.", "Impose a monetary penalty of $100,000 or an amount equal to 75 percent of the appraised value of the merchandise, whichever amount is less."],"Official answer: Liquidate the subject entries at the applicable HTSUS column 1 general rate of duty, in addition to the applicable monetary penalty. Look it up: 19 CFR 163.6(b)(1)(ii); 19 CFR 163.6(b)(2)(i)(A). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 16.)");
Q(9,1,3,"Bob Broker, an individual licensed customs broker, is employed by Fine Furniture, an importer. Fine Furniture makes classification decisions, valuation decisions, prepares entry summaries, and communicates directly with CBP on technical matters. As part of his employment, Bob Broker uses his individual customs broker license, filer code, and national permit to file customs entries for Fine Furniture's imported merchandise. Fine Furniture compensates Bob Broker as a salaried employee. CBP has determined that Fine Furniture is conducting \"customs business\" without an organizational broker’s license, and Bob Broker is allowing his individual broker’s license to be used by an unlicensed entity to transact “customs business.” This arrangement has been ongoing for 10 separate import transactions. What is the maximum civil penalty that CBP could assess against Fine Furniture for Fine Furniture’s violations?","$30,000.00",["$60,000.00", "$100,000.00", "$200,000.00"],"Official answer: $30,000.00 Look it up: 111.4; 19 CFR 111.37; 19 CFR 111.53 (c). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 17.)");
Q(9,5,2,"Three of the entry types below may be sent to the quota subsystem for processing. Which entry type will NOT be sent to the quota subsystem to process?","34",["32", "38", "52"],"Official answer: 34 Look it up: ACE BRPD Section 7.1. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 26.)");
Q(9,5,3,"Global Imports Inc. (Global Imports) has several shipments of refined sugar arriving at the Ports of Los Angeles, CA, and Houston, TX. Due to high demand, the quota for this product oversubscribes at the moment of opening, and CBP announces a proration. Global Imports’ aggregate presented quantity across both ports exceeds its allocated share. To optimize its inventory and distribution, it decides to utilize the \"transfer of allotment\" option, taking a larger portion of its prorated quantity at Los Angeles and a smaller portion at Houston, ensuring it does not exceed its total allowed allocation. Global Imports’ broker should contact _____ to request a transfer of allotment. Information pertaining to this request must be provided to CBP within _____ business days of authorized release of the shipment.","HQ Quota; 5",["Designated Center of Excellence and Expertise; 15", "Designated Center of Excellence and Expertise; 5", "HQ Quota; 15"],"Official answer: HQ Quota; 5 Look it up: ACE BRPD Section 7.12; 19 CFR 132.13. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 27.)");
Q(9,5,3,"ABC Customs Brokerage located in Miami, Florida is preparing an entry for a shipment to arrive into the port of Los Angeles, California containing flue-cured tobacco from the Republic of South Africa. This tobacco falls under the highly competitive \"other countries\" category of the Tariff Rate Quota (TRQ), which is quickly filling. Logistics provider, Mega Transport, located in Carson, California transmits the arrival notice into ACE with an estimated arrival date of Sunday, May 24, 2026, at 5:00 p.m. Pacific Time. Due to a weather delay, the shipment didn’t arrive into the Port of Los Angeles until Monday, May 25, 2026, at 8:30 a.m. Pacific Time. As a result, Mega Transport re-transmits the actual arrival date and time into ACE. On May 26, 2026, at 5:00 p.m. Eastern Time ABC Customs Brokerage transmits and CBP receives an error free entry and entry summary on daily statement. What is the correct quota presentation date and time for this entry? *Highlighted Dates = Federal Holidays","Tuesday, May 26, 2026 at 2:00 p.m. Pacific Time",["Sunday, May 24, 2026, at 5:00 p.m. Pacific Time", "Monday, May 25, 2026, at 8:30 a.m. Pacific Time", "Wednesday, May 27, 2026 at 8:30 a.m. Pacific Time"],"Official answer: Tuesday, May 26, 2026 at 2:00 p.m. Pacific Time Look it up: 19 CFR 132.3; ACE BRPD Section 7.6. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 28.)");
Q(9,5,3,"Oceanic Canners Inc. (Oceanic Canners), located in Virginia Beach, Virginia specializes in importing canned tuna, which is subject to quota and the “opening moment” procedures. Oceanic Canners instructs its broker to enter its entire shipment of canned tuna for consumption regardless of the duty rate. For the 2026 quota period (January 1, 2026, to December 31, 2026), its customs broker prepares an entry for a large shipment of canned tuna. The quota for this product opens on Friday, January 2, 2026. On this opening day, the total quantity of canned tuna presented by all importers to CBP significantly exceeds the final quota limit for the year. Which of the following best describes how Oceanic Canners’ entry will be processed for quota purposes?","Oceanic Canners will receive a prorated portion of its requested quantity at the in- quota duty rate, with the remainder subject to the higher over-quota duty rate.",["Oceanic Canners’ entry will be rejected entirely, and it must re-submit once the quota reopens.", "Oceanic Canners will receive its full quantity requested at the in-quota duty rate because its entry was submitted on the opening day.", "Oceanic Canners’ entry will be held until the end of the quota period to determine if any remaining quota is available."],"Official answer: Oceanic Canners will receive a prorated portion of its requested quantity at the in- quota duty rate, with the remainder subject to the higher over-quota duty rate. Look it up: ACE BRPD Section 7.8; 19 CFR 132.13. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 29.)");
Q(9,5,2,"Best Beef LLC (Best Beef) plans to import beef from Australia in 2026 under a tariff-rate quota. To claim the in-quota duty rate, Best Beef must have a valid export certificate and must report the certificate’s unique identifying number. Australia issued certificate number 123456. Under the applicable requirements, which answer choice shows the correct order of the elements in the export certificate’s unique identifying number?","6AU123456",["6123456AU", "AU6123456", "123456AU6 Category IV - Classification"],"Official answer: 6AU123456 Look it up: 19 CFR 132.15(b). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 30.)");
Q(9,3,2,"Of the four choices below, which is the best CLASSIFICATION of a set of steel hand- tools that includes a screwdriver, an adjustable wrench, slip joint pliers, and a handsaw? The tools are imported and packaged for retail sale in a plastic blister pack.","8206.00.00",["8203.20.40", "8204.12.00", "8205.90.60"],"Official answer: 8206.00.00 Look it up: HTSUS Chapter 82. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 31.)");
Q(9,3,2,"Of the four choices below, which is the best CLASSIFICATION of an integrated rear automotive lighting module designed for a standard 4-door gasoline-powered passenger sedan? The assembly contains an incandescent brake light, a LED turn signal, and a dedicated incandescent reversing (backup) light, all housed within a single plastic casing. The component is imported as an individual replacement part without any wiring harnesses or electronic control units.","8512.20.40",["8512.20.20", "8512.90.20", "8708.99.81"],"Official answer: 8512.20.40 Look it up: HTSUS 8512.20.40; Section XVII, Note 2(f). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 32.)");
Q(9,3,2,"Of the four choices below, which is the best CLASSIFICATION for a woven fabric composed of man-made synthetic fibers? The fabric is woven with yarns of different colors and is composed of 84 percent polyester (of which 90 percent are staple fibers and 10 percent are filament yarns) and 16 percent viscose rayon staple fibers. A polyvinyl chloride plastic coating has been applied to the face of the fabric, which is not visible to the naked eye.","5515.11.00",["5407.93.20", "5514.11.00", "5903.10.25"],"Official answer: 5515.11.00 Look it up: 2(a)(1) to Chapter 59. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 33.)");
Q(9,3,2,"Of the four choices below, which heading should be used to classify diammonium hydrogenorthophosphate, which contains 18% nitrogen and 46% phosphorus?","Heading 3105",["Heading 2805", "Heading 3102", "Heading 3104"],"Official answer: Heading 3105 Look it up: HTSUS Chapter 31, Note 5. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 34.)");
Q(9,3,2,"Of the four choices below, what is the best CLASSIFICATION of organic cassava flat bread? It is 100% cassava flour, containing no wheat flour, cocoa, sugar, milk, meat, or seafood. The manufacturing process entails peeling, grating, dewatering, and steaming. It is imported packaged for retail sale, with 10 slices per 10-ounce package.","2008.99.65",["1106.30.90", "1901.90.91", "1903.00.20"],"Official answer: 2008.99.65 Look it up: Heading 2008. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 35.)");
Q(9,3,2,"Of the four choices below, which is the best CLASSIFICATION of a pair of unisex “Slip-On Duster Slippers,” designed to be worn on the feet to wipe or sweep dust from the floor from a standing position and composed of polyester woven fabric tufted with polyester chenille yarn, without an applied outer sole?","6307.10.20",["6307.90.98", "6405.20.30", "6405.20.90"],"Official answer: 6307.10.20 Look it up: HTSUS Chapter 64, Note 1(b). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 36.)");
Q(9,3,2,"Of the four choices below, which is the best CLASSIFICATION of a fixed-blade knife intended for use in hunting? The knife features a stainless-steel blade with an acrylonitrile butadiene styrene (ABS) plastic handle and comes in a substantial leather holster.","8211.92.40",["8211.91.50", "8211.92.60", "8211.92.90"],"Official answer: 8211.92.40 Look it up: HTSUS Chapter 82, Statistical Note 2. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 37.)");
Q(9,3,3,"Of the four choices below, which is the best CLASSIFICATION of a steel hairspring (balance spring)? It is a small, slender, flat-coiled spring that regulates a mechanical watch's weighted balance wheel. It causes the wheel to oscillate back and forth in a pendulum-like motion several times per second, which controls the movement of the watch's hands automatically without human intervention. The mechanical watches that use these hairsprings do not contain any electrical connectors, insulators, coils, contacts, or other electrical features.","9114.90.34",["7320.90.10", "9110.19.00", "9111.90.70"],"Official answer: 9114.90.34 Look it up: HTSUS Chapter 91 Note 1(c) and additional U.S. Note 1(g). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 38.)");
Q(9,3,2,"Of the four choices below, which is the best CLASSIFICATION of imitation precious or semiprecious stones made of glass which are designed to be used in jewelry?","7018.10.20",["7018.90.50", "7103.99.10", "7104.99.10"],"Official answer: 7018.10.20 Look it up: HTSUS Chapter 70, Additional U.S. Note 5. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 39.)");
Q(9,3,3,"Of the four descriptions of merchandise below, which merchandise is eligible for the duty treatment provided under subheading 9802.00.80 HTSUS upon importation into the United States? The merchandise is an incandescent-bulb chandelier assembled abroad. The chandelier components being assembled are: a brass frame constructed of shaped brass tubing, a brass chain made of oval links, a wiring loom, bulb sockets, pentagon- shaped glass to fit in the sides of the brass frame. Upon importation, the chandelier will be classified under 9405.19.4010 with a duty rate of 3.9%. The components are being assembled at a plant in Thailand (\"the plant\").","The U.S. importer (importer) exported chandelier kits of all the components to the plant. The importer manufactured the brass tubing and oval links in the United States from brass imported from Germany. The importer manufactured the glass panels from sand and sodium carbonate imported from Brazil. The importer purchased the wiring loom and bulb sockets from a U.S. manufacturer. The plant assembles the kit into a finished chandelier for export to the United States.",["The U.S. importer supplied the plant, free of charge, the molds for fabricating the pentagon-shaped glass and for fabricating the brass tubing and chain links. The brass and glass raw materials are obtained by the plant from a business in Thailand. The wiring loom and bulb sockets are imported by the plant from China. All the components, once created, are assembled into a finished chandelier for export to the United States.", "The U.S. importer exported chandelier kits of all the components to the plant. The brass tubing and chain links were fabricated in Germany, the bulb sockets and wiring loom were imported from Barbados, and the glass panels were imported from France. The plant assembles the kit into a finished chandelier for export to the United States.", "The U.S. importer sold the plant the molds for fabricating the pentagon-shaped glass and for fabricating the brass tubing and chain links. The U.S.-origin sand, sodium carbonate, and brass (copper-zinc alloy) are purchased by the plant and imported to Thailand. The U.S. importer manufactures the wiring loom and bulb sockets and exports them to the plant. The plant uses the molds to manufacture the glass panels and brass tubes and links. The plant assembles all the components into a finished chandelier for export to the United States."],"Official answer: The U.S. importer (importer) exported chandelier kits of all the components to the plant. The importer manufactured the brass tubing and oval links in the United States from brass imported from Germany. The importer manufactured the glass panels from sand and sodium carbonate imported from Brazil. The importer purchased the wiring loom and bulb sockets from a U.S. manufacturer. The plant assembles the kit into a finished chandelier for export to the United States. Look it up: HTSUS 9802.00.80. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 40.)");
Q(9,2,2,"Which of the following does NOT qualify for an exception to the country-of-origin marking requirements?","Articles marked with the name of a location in the United States, intended as part of a souvenir marking",["Products of the United States exported and returned", "Articles produced more than 20 years prior to their importation into the United States", "Articles imported for use by the importer and not intended for sale in its imported form or any other form"],"Official answer: Articles marked with the name of a location in the United States, intended as part of a souvenir marking Look it up: 19 CFR 134.47; 19 CFR 134 Subpart D.. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 41.)");
Q(9,2,2,"Which of the following is NOT an example of “minor processing” when determining the country of origin of imported goods from Canada or Mexico?","Mounting components such as switches and lights in an enclosure",["Cleaning, including removal of rust, grease, paint, or other coatings", "Testing, marking, sorting, or grading", "Trimming, filing or cutting off small amounts of excess material"],"Official answer: Mounting components such as switches and lights in an enclosure Look it up: 19 CFR 102.1(n). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 42.)");
Q(9,2,3,"Natty Knickers, Inc. (NK Inc.) submitted a purchase order to a trouser manufacturer for 100 dozen women's woven cotton pajama pants. The manufacturer purchases cotton yarn, grown and spun in Brazil. The yarn is shipped to Turkey where it is woven into fabric and tie-dyed. The tie-dye fabric goes to Vietnam where it is cut and sewn into pajama pants. The pajama pants are then sent to Mexico, where they are washed, packaged, and labeled. NK Inc. imports the pajama pants into the U.S. What is the country of origin of the pajama pants?","Vietnam",["Brazil", "Mexico", "Turkey"],"Official answer: Vietnam Look it up: 19 CFR 134.1(b); 19 CFR 102.21(c)(3)(ii). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 43.)");
Q(9,2,2,"Which ONE of the following statements is TRUE regarding an owner or lessee’s application to establish a bonded warehouse facility?","If the proposed warehouse is covered by fire insurance, the applicant shall submit evidence of the coverage.",["The applicant shall state whether the warehouse facility is to be operated as a public bonded warehouse, only if the application is for a class 2 warehouse.", "The applicant shall state the general character of the merchandise to be stored therein, only if the warehouse facility is to be operated as a public bonded warehouse.", "The applicant shall prepare and have available at the warehouse a certification stating that the inventory control and recordkeeping system meets the requirements of 19 CFR 19.12, but this certification is not to be submitted with the application."],"Official answer: If the proposed warehouse is covered by fire insurance, the applicant shall submit evidence of the coverage. Look it up: 19 CFR 19.2(b). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 44.)");
Q(9,2,2,"Which ONE of the four choices below describes merchandise that may be granted domestic status within a Foreign Trade Zone (FTZ)?","Previously imported merchandise on which duty and tax have been paid",["Foreign merchandise that has been manipulated in a zone and is being transferred back into Customs territory", "Merchandise admitted into a zone under a Customs Form 214 requiring a formal application and permit", "Merchandise in zone-restricted status that was previously abandoned to the government"],"Official answer: Previously imported merchandise on which duty and tax have been paid Look it up: 19 CFR 146.32(a)(2); 19 CFR 146.44(a). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 45.)");
Q(9,2,2,"Speedy Transport has delivered an in-bond shipment to the foreign trade zone (FTZ) for Widgets Inc. Upon inspection of the high-security seal at time of unloading, it is determined that the seal has been compromised, cut, and reattached. After reporting the compromised seal to the port director, what shall the FTZ operator do next?","Hold the vehicle or container and its contents intact pending instructions from the port director.",["Return the shipment back to the arrival port.", "Prepare a certificate to waive the sealing requirement in accordance with 19 CFR 18.4(2).", "Affix the merchandise with a warning label or tag in accordance with 19 CFR 18.4(b)(3) before admitting the shipment into the FTZ."],"Official answer: Hold the vehicle or container and its contents intact pending instructions from the port director. Look it up: 19 CFR 146.8. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 46.)");
Q(9,2,2,"Which of the following is TRUE when reporting consignee information on an entry summary?","Consignee information reported on the entry summary must be maintained for cargo release and Importer Security Filing (ISF) purposes.",["The consignee's social security number (SSN) must only be reported when the importer of record is a foreign entity.", "The consignee's address is not reported on an entry summary.", "Consignee information must be reported on the entry summary only if it is different from the importer of record."],"Official answer: Consignee information reported on the entry summary must be maintained for cargo release and Importer Security Filing (ISF) purposes. Look it up: ACE Entry Summary Instructions, page 8. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 47.)");
Q(9,2,2,"In which scenario is the Surety Code \"999\" used when filing an entry summary?","When the bond is waived by regulation",["When a cash deposit is used in place of a bond", "When a single transaction bond amount is unknown", "When a continuous bond is not applicable"],"Official answer: When the bond is waived by regulation Look it up: ACE Entry Summary Instructions, page 3. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 48.)");
Q(9,2,2,"A customs broker is preparing an ACE entry summary for merchandise arriving by truck. What should the broker report as the U.S. Port of Unlading?","No port of unlading; the field should be left blank",["The port of entry where the truck arrives", "The port where the merchandise is released", "The port nearest to the ultimate consignee"],"Official answer: No port of unlading; the field should be left blank Look it up: ACE Entry Summary Instructions, page 10. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 49.)");
Q(9,2,2,"An importer brings commercial samples into the United States under an A.T.A. carnet. Six months after the carnet's issuance, the carnet is physically destroyed in a warehouse fire before the merchandise can be reexported. The importer requests a replacement carnet from the association that issued the original carnet abroad. CBP may NOT accept the replacement carnet if which of the following statements is TRUE?","The period of validity for the replacement carnet is set to expire one year from the date of its issuance.",["The replacement carnet includes a description of the commercial samples that exactly matches the description set forth in the importation voucher from the original carnet.", "The commercial samples under the original carnet are physically located within the Customs territory of the United States at the time of the request for a replacement carnet.", "Upon request for the replacement carnet, CBP discovers that the commercial samples are eligible for Temporary Importation under Bond."],"Official answer: The period of validity for the replacement carnet is set to expire one year from the date of its issuance. Look it up: 19 CFR 114.23; 19 CFR 114.25. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 50.)");
Q(9,2,2,"Which ONE of the four choices below correctly describes the calculation of liquidated damages when there is an anticipatory breach of a temporary importation under bond (TIB)?","110 percent of the estimated duties applicable to such an entry, if the subject articles are samples solely for use in taking orders",["Two times the estimated duties applicable to such an entry, if the subject articles originated in Portugal and were imported for sports purposes by a Canadian national", "Two times the estimated duties applicable to such an entry, if the subject articles are professional equipment and repair components thereof", "Three times the estimated duties applicable to such an entry, if the subject articles were entered under a carnet"],"Official answer: 110 percent of the estimated duties applicable to such an entry, if the subject articles are samples solely for use in taking orders Look it up: 10.39(d)(3), 19 CFR 10.39(f). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 51.)");
Q(9,2,2,"If no law or regulation expressly provides that a lesser amount may be taken, which ONE of the CBP bond amounts is acceptable?","$125.00",["$80.00", "$99.99", "$150.50"],"Official answer: $125.00 Look it up: 19 CFR 113.13(a). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 52.)");
Q(9,2,2,"An unlicensed importation of merchandise is subject to the Treasury Department's Office of Foreign Assets Control (OFAC) regulations and detained at the port of entry. OFAC determines that the merchandise is prohibited by economic sanctions and does NOT authorize immediate re-exportation or other disposition of the merchandise. In this scenario, what shall happen to the prohibited merchandise?","The merchandise shall be seized without entry for any purpose.",["The merchandise shall be entered into OFAC possession and held with penalty.", "The merchandise shall be entered into CBP possession and held with penalty.", "The merchandise shall be subject to additional duties and penalties upon entry."],"Official answer: The merchandise shall be seized without entry for any purpose. Look it up: 19 CFR 12.150(b). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 53.)");
Q(9,2,2,"A shipment of pesticides arrives in the United States, but the importer fails to present a completed Notice of Arrival (NOA) form to CBP concurrently with the entry documentation. What is the maximum initial period this shipment will be detained by CBP, and within how many calendar days after the expiration of the detention period without extension must the consignee export the shipment to avoid destruction by CBP?","30 calendar days; 90 calendar days",["10 working days; 30 calendar days", "30 calendar days; 60 calendar days", "10 working days; 90 calendar days"],"Official answer: 30 calendar days; 90 calendar days Look it up: 19 CFR 12.113. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 54.)");
Q(9,2,2,"Which ONE of the following entry type changes may be made via a post correction summary (PSC)?","A change from entry type 01 to 03",["A change from entry type 02 to 23", "A change from entry type 31 to 32", "A change from entry type 01 to 02"],"Official answer: A change from entry type 01 to 03 Look it up: ACE BRPD 6.6. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 56.)");
Q(9,2,2,"When submitting a post summary correction (PSC), the original entry summary or previously filed PSC must meet which of the following requirements?","In accepted status, not under CBP review, in CBP control, fully paid, and cannot be liquidated",["In accepted status, under CBP review and control, partially paid, and fully liquidated", "In entered status, under CBP review, in CBP control, at least partially paid, and liquidated", "In review status, not under CBP review or control, fully paid, and cannot be liquidated"],"Official answer: In accepted status, not under CBP review, in CBP control, fully paid, and cannot be liquidated Look it up: ACE BRPD 6.2. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 57.)");
Q(9,2,2,"If a customs broker makes entry on behalf of a nominal consignee, who must appear as the importer of record according to Section 484, Tariff Act of 1930?","The customs broker",["The nominal consignee", "The Port Director", "The ultimate purchaser of the goods"],"Official answer: The customs broker Look it up: Right to Make Entry Directive 3530-002A Section 5.1.3. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 58.)");
Q(9,2,2,"Which one of the following entry types liquidates in 24 hours, cannot be cancelled, and is ineligible for Post Summary Correction (PSC)?","Informal Entry",["Consumption Entry", "Consumption-Antidumping/Countervailing Duty Entry", "Warehouse Entry"],"Official answer: Informal Entry Look it up: 19 CFR 128.24; ACE BRPD Section 20.2. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 59.)");
Q(9,2,2,"Absent any exception or extension, how will an entry that has not liquidated within one year from the date of entry of the merchandise, or the date of final withdrawal of all merchandise covered by a warehouse entry, become “deemed liquidated”?","By operation of law",["By certification of the Center director", "By importer’s request", "By binding ruling"],"Official answer: By operation of law Look it up: ACE BRPD Section 1.19; 19 CFR 159.11(a). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 60.)");
Q(9,2,3,"Sure Customs Brokers (Sure) filed a type 01 Consumption Entry for a shipment of ceramic pottery from Japan on behalf of their client Kyoto Clay Merchants. The date of export for the shipment was August 10, 2026, and the shipment arrived within U.S. port limits on August 12, 2026. Sure filed entry documentation (CBP Form 3461 equivalent) on August 12, 2026, and CBP released the merchandise that same day. Then, Sure filed entry summary (CBP Form 7501) and fully paid estimated duties and fees on August 13, 2026. Sure noted that the entry was in accepted status, under CBP control, and not under CBP review as of August 14, 2026. The certified daily exchange rate was 2% less than the certified quarterly rate for Japan on August 10, 2026, and 6% less on August 12, 2026. The Secretary of the Treasury proclaimed a rate which is 5% greater than the certified quarterly rate for Japan on August 13-17, 2026. Sure used the correct exchange rate specified in CBP’s regulations found in 19 CFR Part 159 to report the entered value of the merchandise. On August 17, 2026, Sure realized that the HTSUS classification for the shipment of ceramic pottery had been entered incorrectly on the entry summary documentation. The entry has not been liquidated. Of the four choices below, what is the best course of action Sure can take to remedy the incorrect entry summary documentation?","File a Post Summary Correction (PSC) to amend the classification. Since the certified quarterly exchange rate was used on the original entry summary documentation, Sure will not seek to amend the certified quarterly exchange rate.",["Recall the entry and entry summary documentation. Request an administrative refund and then re-file accurate entry summary for the shipment. File the new entry summary on August 17, 2026, with the entry summary serving as both the entry documentation and entry summary using the proclaimed exchange rate.", "Recall the entry and entry summary documentation. Request an administrative refund and then re-file accurate entry summary for the shipment. File the new entry summary on August 17, 2026, using the certified daily rate on the new entry summary document.", "File a Post Summary Correction (PSC) to amend the classification. Since the certified daily exchange rate was used on the original entry summary documentation, Sure will not seek to amend the certified daily exchange rate."],"Official answer: File a Post Summary Correction (PSC) to amend the classification. Since the certified quarterly exchange rate was used on the original entry summary documentation, Sure will not seek to amend the certified quarterly exchange rate. Look it up: and Section 1.18. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 61.)");
Q(9,2,2,"A trademark owner is submitting an application to record two of their federally registered trademarks. The trademark owner applies to record each trademark for two classes of goods. How much will the applicant pay in total for these initial recordation applications?","$760.00",["$190.00", "$380.00", "$570.00"],"Official answer: $760.00 Look it up: 19 CFR 133.3(b). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 62.)");
Q(9,2,2,"CBP has notified the importer, in writing, of the detention of merchandise suspected of bearing a counterfeit version of a registered mark. How long is the period within which the importer must respond?","7 business days",["3 business days", "5 business days", "10 business days"],"Official answer: 7 business days Look it up: 19 CFR 133.21(b)(2)(ii). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 63.)");
Q(9,2,2,"What are manufacturing drawback claimants under 19 USC 1313(a) or (b) required to certify, regarding the applicable bill of materials or formula?","The bill of materials or formula identifies the manufactured quantities of the imported and/or substituted merchandise and the exported or destroyed article(s).",["The claimant is in possession of the applicable bill of materials or formula for the imported article(s), which will be promptly destroyed upon CBP request.", "The bill of materials or formula identifies the imported and/or substituted merchandise and the exported or destroyed article(s) by their 4-digit HTSUS heading numbers.", "The bill of materials or formula identifies the manufacturer of the imported and/or substituted merchandise and the recipient of the exported or destroyed merchandise."],"Official answer: The bill of materials or formula identifies the manufactured quantities of the imported and/or substituted merchandise and the exported or destroyed article(s). Look it up: 19 CFR 190.29. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 64.)");
Q(9,2,3,"Jane Sibley, of Sibley Furniture Outlet, needs to destroy merchandise pursuant to an unused merchandise drawback claim under 19 USC 1313(j). Ms. Sibley filed a Notice of Intent to Export, Destroy, or Return Merchandise for Purposes of Drawback on CBP Form 7553, notifying CBP that she intends to destroy the merchandise on the seventh working day after CBP’s receipt of her notice. By the date when the merchandise is set to be destroyed, Ms. Sibley had not received notice of CBP’s decision to witness or not witness the destruction. Ms. Sibley proceeds with the destruction of the merchandise as scheduled, without a determination from CBP. Based on the facts above, which ONE of the following statements is TRUE?","The merchandise must be destroyed to the extent that it has no commercial value. Additionally, the destruction may include a process by which materials are recovered from imported merchandise or from an article manufactured from imported merchandise.",["Ms. Sibley violated CBP regulations by proceeding with the destruction of merchandise subject to a drawback claim without written approval from CBP.", "The merchandise will be deemed to have been destroyed under CBP supervision. As the drawback claimant, Ms. Sibley has no further obligation to certify the destruction of the merchandise.", "Ms. Sibley or another officer of Sibley Furniture Outlet must issue evidence of the destruction on CBP Form 7553."],"Official answer: The merchandise must be destroyed to the extent that it has no commercial value. Additionally, the destruction may include a process by which materials are recovered from imported merchandise or from an article manufactured from imported merchandise. Look it up: 19 CFR 190.71. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 65.)");
Q(9,4,2,"The Department of Commerce receives petitions from United States domestic industries to investigate claims of imported goods being sold at less than fair value in United States markets. During this process, what is the first point at which a remedy such as antidumping or countervailing duties typically goes into effect?","At the preliminary determination of an investigation",["At the initiation of an investigation", "At the final determination of an investigation", "At the sunset review of the antidumping and/or countervailing duty order"],"Official answer: At the preliminary determination of an investigation Look it up: 19 CFR 351.205(a) and (c). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 66.)");
Q(9,4,3,"CBP received an allegation on July 1, 2026, from a member of the public against Star Merchant (Star) for the evasion of antidumping duties (AD) on steel products that were made on August 30, 2025, and began an investigation consistent with Enforce and Protect Act (EAPA) orders. CBP has imposed interim measures to protect revenue and issued a Request for Information (RFI) to Star, demanding a description of the covered merchandise as well as operations data, including detailed production records, supply chain maps, and pricing agreements. Star is a client of Gotham Brokers (Gotham), who is authorized to conduct customs business on behalf of Star under a power of attorney with unlimited authority. Star notifies Gotham that it considers all of the information CBP requested to be highly confidential trade secrets and instructs Gotham to redact all documents provided in response to the RFI. Of the four choices below, what is the best advice for Gotham to give Star to prepare a response to the RFI in compliance with CBP regulations?","Gotham advises Star that descriptions of covered merchandise may not be redacted or otherwise treated as “business confidential,” but Star may request that CBP treat its operations data as business confidential. Gotham also provides Star with instructions to prepare business confidential and public versions of the requested documents.",["Gotham advises Star to clearly mark the requested documents with a cover page, stating that the submission contains confidential information, and to enclose the covered merchandise descriptions and operations data within brackets and replace the bracketed information with a sufficiently detailed summary that maintains the confidentiality of Star’s trade secrets.", "Gotham advises Star to request that CBP treat its entire submission in response to the RFI as business confidential. Gotham also provides Star with instructions to prepare both a confidential and a public version of the requested documents.", "Gotham advises Star that no response to the RFI is required because the allegation was not timely made."],"Official answer: Gotham advises Star that descriptions of covered merchandise may not be redacted or otherwise treated as “business confidential,” but Star may request that CBP treat its operations data as business confidential. Gotham also provides Star with instructions to prepare business confidential and public versions of the requested documents. Look it up: 19 CFR 165.2; 19 CFR 165.4; 19 CFR 165.6; 19 CFR 165.26. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 67.)");
Q(9,4,2,"Which program indicator for products eligible for special tariff treatment is used to claim the Column 1 duty preference under the United States-Morocco Free Trade Agreement?","MA",["MO", "MR", "MC"],"Official answer: MA Look it up: HTSUS General Note 27(a). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 68.)");
Q(9,4,2,"The United States-Mexico-Canada Agreement (USMCA) Duty Deferral Entry Program represents a two-part process. What is the deadline for submitting the second part, the claim portion, representing the USMCA country entry information related to the exportation reported in the first part?","It is due within 60 calendar days of export.",["It is due within 10 calendar days of export.", "It is due within 15 calendar days of export.", "It is due within 90 calendar days of export."],"Official answer: It is due within 60 calendar days of export. Look it up: 182.53(a)(2)(iii)(C). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 69.)");
Q(9,4,2,"Which ONE of the following articles qualifies for preferential tariff treatment under the Caribbean Basin Economic Recovery Act (CBERA)?","Rum that is, by value, 100% Puerto Rican content. The rum is shipped to the Bahamas to add Bahamas-origin coconut flavor, bottled, and later exported from the Bahamas to the United States.",["Hula hoops that are, by value, 25% United States content and 10% Aruban content.", "Beach umbrellas that are, by value, 15% Jamaican content and 15% Costa Rican content.", "Souvenir mugs that are, by value, 40% Haitian content that is shipped to Bermuda for warehousing outside customs control and later exported from Bermuda to the United States."],"Official answer: Rum that is, by value, 100% Puerto Rican content. The rum is shipped to the Bahamas to add Bahamas-origin coconut flavor, bottled, and later exported from the Bahamas to the United States. Look it up: 7(b)(v). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 70.)");
Q(9,4,2,"Which ONE of the following amounts is included in transaction value, if it is related to the imported merchandise and required as a condition of the sale for exportation to the United States?","Royalties",["Buying commissions", "U.S. inland freight", "Ocean freight cost"],"Official answer: Royalties Look it up: 19 CFR 152.103(b)(iv). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 71.)");
Q(9,4,2,"If engineering work is necessary for the production of the imported merchandise, it may be an assist for the purposes of appraisement in which ONE of the following scenarios?","The engineering work is performed by an individual domiciled in Canada who is a U.S. citizen and works for a U.S. firm under contract with the U.S. importer.",["The engineering work is performed under contract with the U.S. importer by an individual temporarily domiciled within the United States on assignment by his employer in the Netherlands.", "The engineering work is performed under contract with the U.S. importer by an individual domiciled within the United States and works from home for a U.S.- based firm.", "The engineering work is performed by an individual who resides in Ireland but is temporarily in the United States working as an employee of the U.S. importer."],"Official answer: The engineering work is performed by an individual domiciled in Canada who is a U.S. citizen and works for a U.S. firm under contract with the U.S. importer. Look it up: 19 CFR 152.102(a)(2)(i). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 72.)");
Q(9,4,3,"A U.S. Importer pays $8,953.00 to Brush Factory for a shipment of brooms and mops. The $8,953.00 payment consists of $5,498.00 for the product and $3,455.00 for ocean freight and insurance. Brush Factory would have charged a total of $15,860.00 for the shipment, including the ocean freight and insurance; however, Brush Factory billed a lesser amount because it was indebted to the importer from a past transaction. Based on the facts above, what is the transaction value of the shipment of brooms and mops?","$12,405.00",["$5,498.00", "$8,953.00", "$15,860.00"],"Official answer: $12,405.00 Look it up: 19 CFR 152.103(a)(2). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 73.)");
Q(9,4,3,"A foreign manufacturer sells industrial equipment to an unrelated U.S. buyer. The sales contract states that the buyer must purchase both the equipment and installation-related technical assistance as part of the same transaction. The technical assistance will be performed entirely in the United States after importation. The contract and commercial invoice separately identify each charge. The terms of sale are cost, insurance and freight (CIF) Long Beach, and the invoice lists: • Equipment price: $10,000.00 • International ocean freight: $5,000.00 • Marine insurance: $2,500.00 • Technical assistance performed in the United States after importation: $10,000.00 Which amount should be reported as the transaction value of the imported merchandise?","$10,000.00, because the separately stated international ocean freight, marine insurance, and post-importation technical assistance are not included.",["$17,500.00, because CIF charges must be included in transaction value, but post- importation technical assistance is excluded.", "$20,000.00, because the equipment cannot be purchased without the technical assistance.", "$27,500.00, because all amounts paid under a mandatory sales contract are included in transaction value."],"Official answer: $10,000.00, because the separately stated international ocean freight, marine insurance, and post-importation technical assistance are not included. Look it up: 19 CFR 152.103(i)(2). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 74.)");
Q(9,4,3,"Tumbler USA imports a specialized mold from Spain with all duties, taxes, and fees paid. The mold, valued at $2,000.00, is then used in the production of goods in the United States. Five years after importing the mold, Tumbler USA sells the mold to company Mugs with Attitude (Mugs). Mugs exports the mold to a company in Canada for use in the production of goods in Canada. The Canadian company pays Mugs a rental fee for use of the mold. The rental of the mold is limited to no more than 3 years. The Canadian company uses the mold for 2.5 years to produce goods, does not change the mold in any way, and then ships it back to Mugs in the United States. The mold is returned to the United States in the same condition in which it was exported. Which ONE of the following statements about the duty associated with the mold is TRUE?","The mold is eligible for duty-free entry under subheading 9801.00.10, HTSUS.",["The mold is dutiable based upon its original $2,000.00 value.", "The mold is dutiable based upon its original value less depreciation.", "The mold is fully dutiable upon importation into the United States from Canada."],"Official answer: The mold is eligible for duty-free entry under subheading 9801.00.10, HTSUS. Look it up: HTSUS 9801.00.10; HTSUS 9801.00.20. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 75.)");
Q(9,4,2,"Which of the following elements is NOT included in the sum used to determine computed value?","Transportation costs",["Packing costs", "Production or fabrication costs", "Profit and general expenses"],"Official answer: Transportation costs Look it up: 19 CFR 152.106(a). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 76.)");
Q(9,4,3,"Western Bowman (Bowman), a large United States retail store chain, wholly owns a factory in Chile. The factory manufactures ornate, wooden arrow shafts and ships them to Bowman in the United States. Because the parties are related and CBP determined that the relationship influenced the transfer price, transaction value is not acceptable. Transaction value of identical or similar merchandise is also unavailable. The imported arrow shafts are not further processed after importation. They are sold in the United States in the condition as imported. The profit and general expenses of the imported arrow shafts are consistent with those reflected in sales in the United States of imported merchandise. CBP appraises the merchandise using deductive value. The following information is available for one importation of arrow shafts. The arrow shafts are imported from Chile on March 1, 2026. Within 90 days after importation, Bowman sells the arrow shafts to unrelated U.S. retail customers at the following prices and quantities: US Selling Price per Dozen Quantity Sold • $42.00 400 dozen • $39.00 700 dozen • $36.00 300 dozen The following costs are incurred: Item Amount per Dozen • Profit and general expenses in the United States $5.50 • Federal excise tax $7.80 • Ocean freight and insurance from Chile to the U.S. $2.25 • Transportation from place of importation to place of $1.50 delivery in U.S. (already included in general expenses) • Arrow shaft designs undertaken in the U.S. and $2.00 supplied free of charge to the Chilean factory What is the deductive value of one dozen arrow shafts?","$23.45",["$19.95", "$20.45", "$21.45"],"Official answer: $23.45 Look it up: 19 CFR 152.105. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 77.)");
Q(9,4,2,"A producer sells copper concentrate to a related buyer. The parties’ sales contract states that the price will be determined by the published market price for copper concentrate on the date of delivery. Neither party can control the published market price. Which statement best describes whether transaction value may be used?","Transaction value may be acceptable if the relationship between the buyer and producer did not influence the price actually paid or payable.",["Transaction value is automatically unacceptable because the buyer and producer are related persons.", "Transaction value is unacceptable because any formula-based price is a condition or consideration that prevents valuation.", "Transaction value must be replaced by a test value, which becomes the transaction value of the material."],"Official answer: Transaction value may be acceptable if the relationship between the buyer and producer did not influence the price actually paid or payable. Look it up: 19 CFR 152.103(j)(1)(iv); 19 CFR 152.103(j)(2). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 78.)");
Q(9,4,3,"ABC Company imports a tire cord quality steel wire rod, properly classified under 7213.91.3011, HTSUS. The country of origin is the United Kingdom, and the item is subject to an antidumping duty cash deposit rate of 147.63%. The item is shipped via air to New York, NY and formally entered into U.S. commerce. What is the total amount of fees and estimated duties if the value of the shipment is $53,429.00, the Merchandise Processing Fee is 0.3464% ($33.56 minimum and $651.50 maximum for the applicable fiscal year), and the Harbor Maintenance Fee is 0.125%?","$79,062.31",["$185.08", "$251.87", "$78,877.23"],"Official answer: $79,062.31 Look it up: Summary Instructions, page 1. (CBP October 2026 exam, question 79.)");
Q(9,4,3,"If the Merchandise Processing Fee (MPF) is 0.3464%, what is the MPF for the shipment described below if the MPF is higher than the minimum and lower than the maximum for the applicable fiscal year? A shipment of lighting parts from Italy is classified under 9405.99.4090, HTSUS, dutiable at 6%, and subject to antidumping duties of 13.19%. The buyer pays a total of $89,000.00 for the goods, which includes $4,500.00 ocean freight, $1,000.00 insurance, $250.00 for customs brokerage fees, $15,000.00 CBP duties and fees, MPF paid, and is delivered duty paid (DDP) to Los Angeles. The buyer pays an additional $500.00 for rail freight from Los Angeles to Kentucky.","$241.25",["$289.24", "$308.30", "$425.00"],"Official answer: $241.25 Look it up: 24.23(b)(1)(i)(A). (CBP October 2026 exam, question 80.)");
Q(9,1,2,"A broker fails to file the triennial status report by March 1 of the reporting year which results in the suspension of their license by operation of law. In addition to filing the triennial status report, what must the broker do to reinstate their license?","Pay the required fee within 60 calendar days of the date of the notice of suspension.",["Pay a fine of $500 within 90 calendar days from the date the original report was due.", "File a protest with CBP within 180 days showing just cause as to why their license should be reinstated.", "File an appeal with CBP within three months of the date of the notice of suspension."],"Official answer: Pay the required fee within 60 calendar days of the date of the notice of suspension. Look it up: 19 CFR 111.30(d)(4). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 1.)");
Q(9,1,2,"Which ONE of the following activities may qualify for one credit of continuing broker education?","Giving a one-hour lecture on duty refund opportunities for educational institutions at a convention for educators and university procurement officers. The lecture and the convention have been approved for continuing broker education credit by a CBP-selected accreditor, prior to being held.",["Viewing a one-hour recording of a CBP webinar offered to prepare registered CBLE examinees for exam-day procedures.", "Attending a one-hour discussion session on the potential use of artificial intelligence in tariff classification. The session is offered during an in-person trade workshop near the Port of Baltimore and is attended by several CBP employees. The discussion leader has stated that the event organizers intend to seek approval for continuing broker education credit for the discussion session after the session concludes.", "One hour spent preparing subject matter for a self-guided, five-month long online course on valuation that commenced at the start of the year. The course culminates in a retention test and has been approved for continuing broker education credit by a CBP-selected accreditor."],"Official answer: Giving a one-hour lecture on duty refund opportunities for educational institutions at a convention for educators and university procurement officers. The lecture and the convention have been approved for continuing broker education credit by a CBP-selected accreditor, prior to being held. Look it up: 19 CFR 111.103(a)-(b); 19 CFR 101.1. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 2.)");
Q(9,1,2,"Smith Logistics LLC (\"Smith Logistics\") is a freight forwarder and licensed customs broker. Smith Logistics has executed a valid power of attorney to authorize its unlicensed employee, Erin Johnson, to sign documents pertaining to customs business on its behalf. Which ONE of the following statements is TRUE for this scenario?","Smith Logistics must provide proof of the existence of the power of attorney to CBP upon CBP's request.",["Smith Logistics must file a statement with the processing Center identifying Erin Johnson as a party authorized to sign documents pertaining to customs business on its behalf.", "Smith Logistics must file a copy of the power of attorney with CBP before Erin Johnson may begin signing documents pertaining to customs business on its behalf.", "Smith Logistics must provide CBP with proof that Erin Johnson is at least 21 years old and is a citizen of the United States before she may begin signing documents pertaining to customs business on its behalf."],"Official answer: Smith Logistics must provide proof of the existence of the power of attorney to CBP upon CBP's request. Look it up: 19 CFR 111.2(a)(2)(ii)(A)(1)-(2). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 3.)");
Q(9,1,2,"Torres & Sons, a partnership, executed a power of attorney (POA) with Kent Customs Brokers (KCB) on May 1, 2024. Torres and Sons ended its business relationship with KCB on April 20, 2026, and wishes to also end KCB's POA. Any of the following measures would terminate the POA between Torres and Sons and KCB EXCEPT:","Executing a new POA with a different customs broker.",["Providing a written notice of the POA's revocation to CBP electronically and receiving confirmation of the notice's receipt.", "Waiting until the POA expires on May 1, 2026.", "Forming a new firm by adding an additional member to the partnership."],"Official answer: Executing a new POA with a different customs broker. Look it up: 19 CFR 141.39, 19 CFR 141.34, 19 CFR 141.35. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 4.)");
Q(9,1,2,"Which ONE of the four choices below correctly identifies how long a customs broker must retain the speficied type of record?","Records concerning the filing of a drawback claim must be retained for three years after liquidation of the claim or for a longer period if required by law.",["An entry summary document, or CBP Form 7501, must be retained for three years after the date of entry.", "Records relating to the withdrawal of merchandise from a bonded warehouse must be retained for five years after the date of entry into the bonded warehouse.", "A document or letter by which an importer revokes a previously granted power of attorney to a customs broker must be retained for five years after the execution date of the subject power of attorney."],"Official answer: Records concerning the filing of a drawback claim must be retained for three years after liquidation of the claim or for a longer period if required by law. Look it up: 19 CFR 163.4(a)-(b); 19 CFR 111.23(a)-(b); 19 CFR 190.15. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 5.)");
Q(9,1,2,"Which ONE of the following four scenarios best demonstrates proper compliance with a record examination request? Assume that duly accredited representatives of the U.S. Department of Homeland Security (DHS) made each of the requests below.","A customs broker receives a request to inspect a customs broker’s records relating to an unused merchandise drawback claim. The request specifies that the broker has up to 120 calendar days to provide the requested records. On the 45th calendar day from the request, the broker sends the original electronic version of the requested records to DHS.",["A customs broker receives a request to inspect records relating to a client that the broker stopped working with over five years ago. The broker has maintained the requested records but informs DHS that they will not make the records available for inspection because the period of retention has ended.", "A customs broker receives a request to inspect records relating to a particular entry of merchandise from an entry made two years ago. The broker mails the original paper version of the requested records to DHS, postmarked 60 calendar days after the request was made.", "A customs broker receives reasonable notice of a site visit. The notice includes a request to inspect records pertaining to a client of the brokerage. During the site visit, the broker informs DHS that the records are kept off-site and will not be available for inspection during the visit. The broker states that they will send the records electronically within 30 calendar days of the visit."],"Official answer: A customs broker receives a request to inspect a customs broker’s records relating to an unused merchandise drawback claim. The request specifies that the broker has up to 120 calendar days to provide the requested records. On the 45th calendar day from the request, the broker sends the original electronic version of the requested records to DHS. Look it up: 19 CFR 111.23(a)-(b); 19 CFR 111.25(a)-(b); 19 CFR 163. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 6.)");
Q(9,1,2,"No later than _____ calendar days after initiating an investigation under 19 CFR 165.15, CBP will take interim measures if there is _____ that the importer entered covered merchandise into the customs territory of the United States through evasion.","90; reasonable suspicion",["15; substantial evidence", "15; reasonable suspicion", "90; substantial evidence"],"Official answer: 90; reasonable suspicion Look it up: 19 CFR 165.24(a). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 7.)");
Q(9,1,2,"What is the maximum civil fine CBP may impose after the seizure of merchandise imported for sale when the merchandise is seized pursuant to 19 USC 1526(e) for bearing a counterfeit mark, has a domestic value of $1,000, and would have a manufacturer’s suggested retail price (MSRP) of $25,000 in the United States at the time of seizure if it had been genuine?","$25,000 if this seizure is the importer’s first seizure under 19 USC 1526(e)",["$1,000 if this seizure is the importer’s first seizure under 19 USC 1526(e)", "$1,000 if this seizure is the importer’s second seizure under 19 USC 1526(e)", "$25,000 if this seizure is the importer’s second seizure under 19 USC 1526(e)"],"Official answer: $25,000 if this seizure is the importer’s first seizure under 19 USC 1526(e) Look it up: 19 CFR 133.27; 19 CFR 133.21. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 8.)");
Q(9,1,2,"An individually licensed customs broker voluntarily suspends her license on September 1, 2024, and reinstates her license on November 15, 2028. How many continuing education credits must she complete during the 2027-2030 triennial reporting period?","14",["13", "27", "36"],"Official answer: 14 Look it up: 19 CFR 111.1; 19 CFR 111.102(a)-(b). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 9.)");
Q(9,1,2,"A customs broker discovers that a client has omitted information from an affidavit. The broker believes the client omitted the information by mistake and advises the client promptly of the omission and the proper corrective actions required. What else must the broker do?","Retain a record of their communication with the client.",["Provide the client with the monetary penalty associated with making the omission.", "Tell the client the broker can no longer conduct customs transactions on the client's behalf.", "Suspend their customs broker's license until the client resolves their omission on the affidavit."],"Official answer: Retain a record of their communication with the client. Look it up: 19 CFR 111.39(c). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 10.)");
Q(9,1,2,"Of the four choices below, which business relationship may Tracy, a licensed customs broker and sole proprietor of her brokerage, maintain without violating CBP regulations?","Tracy transacts customs business on behalf of her client, X-Logistics, a bona fide importer. At the time when she entered into the broker-client relationship, Tracy knew that X-Logistics was a notoriously disreputable importer.",["Tracy has an agreement with Faith, another sole proprietor who had her customs broker license suspended with prejudice. For the duration of Faith’s license suspension, Faith refers her clients to Tracy, and Tracy gives Faith a small percentage of the fees collected from the clients Faith referred to her.", "Tracy befriends an attorney, Margaret, at a trade conference. Margaret works for the Office of Regulations and Rulings at the Department of Homeland Security and authors classification rulings for the type of widgets that Tracy’s clients import. Tracy treats Margaret to a nice dinner in exchange for the opportunity to talk about the classification rulings pending Margaret’s review.", "Tracy hires Paul to help with recordkeeping at her brokerage. Six months after Paul’s start date, Tracy discovers that Paul is a convicted felon. Tracy takes no futher action and continues to employ Paul."],"Official answer: Tracy transacts customs business on behalf of her client, X-Logistics, a bona fide importer. At the time when she entered into the broker-client relationship, Tracy knew that X-Logistics was a notoriously disreputable importer. Look it up: 19 CFR 111.42; 19 CFR 111.53; 19 CFR 111.34. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 11.)");
Q(9,1,2,"Which of the following statements regarding national permits is FALSE?","Applicants who receive notification from the appropriate Executive Director, Office of Trade, CBP Headquarters that their national permit application has been denied may appeal the decision to the Court of International Trade directly, provided that the appeal action is commenced within 60 calendar days after the original denial date by the Executive Director.",["A national permit is required for the purpose of transacting customs business throughout the customs territory of the United States.", "Individuals who obtain a passing grade on the Customs Broker License Examination may submit applications for a customs broker license and a national permit at the same time.", "The individual broker who qualifies their business entity for a national permit is required to exercise responsible supervision and control over the activities conducted under that national permit."],"Official answer: Applicants who receive notification from the appropriate Executive Director, Office of Trade, CBP Headquarters that their national permit application has been denied may appeal the decision to the Court of International Trade directly, provided that the appeal action is commenced within 60 calendar days after the original denial date by the Executive Director. Look it up: 19 CFR 111.19. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 12.)");
Q(9,1,2,"When must customs brokers notify CBP of a change in address for their office of record?","Within 10 calendar days of the change",["Within 10 business days of the change", "Within 30 calendar days of the change", "Within 30 business days of the change"],"Official answer: Within 10 calendar days of the change Look it up: 19 CFR 111.30(a). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 13.)");
Q(9,1,3,"As a licensed customs broker and sole proprietor of her business, Janet Martin is responsible for submitting a list of the names of her current employees to the processing Center. Ms. Martin has two employees, Spencer McGill and Logan Sharpe. Ms. Martin submitted Spencer McGill’s relevant information to the processing Center within 30 calendar days of his employment in 2021 and none of Mr. McGill’s information has changed since then. Logan Sharpe is a new employee, and Ms. Martin has not yet notified the processing Center of Ms. Sharpe’s employment. Which ONE of the following lists should Ms. Martin timely provide to the processing Center to best comply with CBP regulations?","NEW EMPLOYEE: Name: Logan Sharpe SSN: 098-76-5432 Date of Birth: October 29, 1979 Place of Birth: Bentonville, AR Date of Hire: April 8, 2026 Current Home Address: 123 Evening Blvd., St. Louis, MO 12345",["Name: Logan Sharpe SSN: 098-76-5432 Date of Birth: 1979 Place of Birth: Bentonville, AR Date of Hire: April 8, 2026 Current Mailing Address: PO Box 789, St. Louis, MO 12345", "Name: Spencer McGill SSN: 123-45-6789 Date of Birth: July 12, 1990 Date of Hire: September 7, 2021 Current Home Address: 123 Morning Ln., St. Louis, MO 12345 Name: Logan Sharpe (NEW EMPLOYEE) SSN: 098-76-5432 Date of Birth: October 29, 1979 Date of Hire: April 8, 2026 Current Home Address: 123 Evening Blvd., St. Louis, MO 12345", "Name: Spencer McGill; 123-45-6789 Date and Place of Birth: July 12, 1990; Washington, DC Date of Hire: September 7, 2021 Current Mailing Address: 123 Morning Ln., St. Louis, MO 12345 Name: Logan Sharpe; 098-76-5432 Date and Place of Birth: October 29, 1979; Bentonville, AR Date of Hire: April 8, 2026 Current Mailing Address: PO Box 789, St. Louis, MO 12345"],"Official answer: NEW EMPLOYEE: Name: Logan Sharpe SSN: 098-76-5432 Date of Birth: October 29, 1979 Place of Birth: Bentonville, AR Date of Hire: April 8, 2026 Current Home Address: 123 Evening Blvd., St. Louis, MO 12345 Look it up: 19 CFR 111.28(b). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 14.)");
Q(9,1,2,"Which of the following documents must be filed with CBP in circumstances where a licensed customs broker's employee is authorized to transact customs business on behalf of the broker?","Statement identifying an employee as authorized to transact customs business on a licensed broker's behalf",["Power of attorney authorizing an employee to sign documents pertaining to customs business on a licensed broker's behalf", "Evidence of employment by the brokerage, attached to each document signed by the employee", "Power of attorney executed with the client, attached to each document signed by the employee"],"Official answer: Statement identifying an employee as authorized to transact customs business on a licensed broker's behalf Look it up: 19 CFR 111.2(a)(2)(ii)(A)(1)-(2). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 15.)");
Q(9,1,2,"In the event of a cyberbreach of electronic records relating to customs business, when must the customs broker report the breach to CBP following the discovery of the breach?","Within 72 hours",["Within 10 calendar days", "Within 10 business days", "Within 30 calendar days"],"Official answer: Within 72 hours Look it up: 19 CFR 111.21(b). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 16.)");
Q(9,1,2,"Jane Smith is a licensed customs broker and a qualifying officer of ABC Broker, Inc. corporation. If ABC Broker, Inc. terminates Jane Smith's employment, which of the following best describes how Jane Smith must report her termination to CBP?","Jane Smith must immediately provide written notice to the appropriate Executive Director, Office of Trade, and must send a copy of the written notice to the processing Center.",["Jane Smith has no obligation to report the termination of her employment. Her former employer, ABC Broker Inc., is responsible for reporting the termination to CBP.", "Jane Smith has up to 30 calendar days to provide written notice to the Executive Assistant Commissioner and the Court of International Trade.", "Jane Smith has up to 30 business days to provide written notice to the processing Center."],"Official answer: Jane Smith must immediately provide written notice to the appropriate Executive Director, Office of Trade, and must send a copy of the written notice to the processing Center. Look it up: 19 CFR 111.28(d). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 17.)");
Q(9,5,3,"Bake 4U, Inc.(B4U) imports bulk pastry dough. The dough is subject to a tariff-rate quota, with an annual aggregate limit of 3,500,000 kg. The 2026 quota period runs from April 1, 2026, through March 30, 2027. B4U sources its pastry dough from the Netherlands, and at importation admits the dough into a Foreign Trade Zone (FTZ) in Texas. The dough is admitted in privileged foreigh status. Within its subzone, B4U operates commercial baking machines in temperature controlled facilities wherein the dough is used to create individually portioned baked pastries that are packaged into designer cartons for bulk sale to hotel and restaurant groups across Texas. B4U files the requisite entry for consumption in order to withdraw the baked pastries from the FTZ. Today, an emergency arose in B4U's subzone due to a failure of the temperature controlled refrigerators. To prevent the dough in those refrigerators from becoming unuseable, B4U decided to immediately bake all of the affected dough in its commercial baking machines. Since this will result in B4U producing a greater amount of pastries than it can package into designer cartons for bulk sale to restaurant and hotel groups, B4U has decided to sell its excess pastries at retail on-site to all persons who work at the FTZ. As of today, the annual dough quota has not yet filled. Under these facts, which of the following answer choices is a TRUE statement?","The pastries B4U withdraws from an FTZ for bulk sale are only eligible for the higher non-quota duty rate in effect on the date privileged foreign status was granted.",["The dough B4U imports is eligible to obtain quota status upon submission of CBP Form 214 in proper form, to achieve admission in privileged foreign status.", "Due to the emergency, B4U may elect nonprivileged foreign status for the affected dough, which is baked into pastries for retail sale within the FTZ.", "The pastries B4U withdraws from an FTZ for bulk sale are eligible to obtain a lower in-quota duty rate upon presentation of an entry summary in proper form, and will be dutiable in accordance with their condition at withdrawal from the FTZ."],"Official answer: The pastries B4U withdraws from an FTZ for bulk sale are only eligible for the higher non-quota duty rate in effect on the date privileged foreign status was granted. Look it up: 19 CFR 132.11(a)-(b); 19 CFR 146.14; 19 CFR 146.41(e); 19. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 27.)");
Q(9,5,2,"A&M Industries entered one entry of peanut butter from India as a type 01 consumption entry on May 1, 2024. Peanut butter from India is subject to a tariff rate quota with an in-quota tariff rate of 0%. The 2024 quota period is January 1, 2024, to December 31, 2024. The quota for this period has closed but it did not fill. On January 31, 2025, A&M Industries wishes to amend the entry summary to change the entry to a type 02 quota entry. Can A&M Industries claim quota for the 2024 quota period?","No, the 2024 quota period has closed.",["No, but A&M Industries can file a post summary correction amending the entry summary to change the entry to a type 02 quota entry and claim quota for the 2025 quota period.", "Yes, A&M Industries should request that CBP cancel the entry so that a replacement quota entry type may be submitted.", "Yes, A&M Industries should contact HQ Quota to arrange for quota reservation in the 2024 quota period."],"Official answer: No, the 2024 quota period has closed. Look it up: ACE BRPD Section 7.14. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 28.)");
Q(9,5,2,"You are a licensed customs broker and have submitted Entry Summary data through the Automated Broker Interface (ABI). After performing preliminary data validations, the system sends you an \"AX\" type message with a REJECT status. Which of the following could NOT have resulted in this rejection?","The entry type is for quota and there is at least one line listing quota goods and the quota is available.",["The merchandise licenses, visas, or certificates are incorrect.", "The quota is full, expired, or closed.", "The quota is not yet opened."],"Official answer: The entry type is for quota and there is at least one line listing quota goods and the quota is available. Look it up: ACE BRPD Section 7.16. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 29.)");
Q(9,5,2,"Which of the following is NOT a method for establishing tariff-rate and absolute quotas?","U.S. Trade Representative orders",["Executive orders", "Presidential proclamations", "Legislative enactments"],"Official answer: U.S. Trade Representative orders Look it up: 19 CFR 132.2(a). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 30.)");
Q(9,3,2,"Of the four choices below, which is the best CLASSIFICATION for dried pineapples that are reduced in size, suitable for immediate consumption, and imported in small packages rather than in bulk?","0804.30.60",["0801.19.01", "0812.90.40", "0813.40.90"],"Official answer: 0804.30.60 Look it up: Heading 0804, HTSUS. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 31.)");
Q(9,3,2,"Of the four choices below, which is the best CLASSIFICATION of alloy steel coils? The cold-rolled steel is successively superimposed in coils and measures 1220 mm in width and 3.75 mm in thickness. The steel chemistry consists of 0.15% Carbon, 0.7 % Titanium, and 25% Nickel. The coils have not been further worked than cold-rolled.","7225.50.8010",["7209.15.0000", "7219.32.0035", "7226.99.0130"],"Official answer: 7225.50.8010 Look it up: 7225.50.8010, HTSUS; HTSUS General Rule of Interpretation. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 32.)");
Q(9,3,2,"Of the four choices below, which is the best CLASSIFICATION of a solenoid valve used to control the airflow within the suspension system of a motor vehicle?","8481.80.9005",["8479.89.9599", "8481.20.0060", "8708.99.8180"],"Official answer: 8481.80.9005 Look it up: 8481.80.9005, HTSUS; GRIs 1 and 6. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 33.)");
Q(9,3,2,"Of the four choices below, which is the best CLASSIFICATION of a men's bow tie constructed of 100% cotton woven black fabric with gray polka dots?","6215.90.0015",["6117.80.2000", "6117.80.8710", "6215.10.0025"],"Official answer: 6215.90.0015 Look it up: 6215.90.0015, HTSUS; Note 1 to Chapter 61; Note 1 to Chapter. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 34.)");
Q(9,3,2,"Which of the following is NOT a requirement with respect to additional articles or classes of articles designated Instruments of International Traffic?","Entry or the payment of duty prior to release, subject to the provisions of the relevant CBP regulation.",["Generally, a container that has been designated an Instrument of International Traffic must exit the United States within 365 days of the date on which it was admitted for it to be deemed to remain in international traffic.", "Normal accessories and equipment must be imported with a “container” that qualifies as an Instrument of International Traffic to, themselves, be included in “instruments of international traffic.”", "Filing of a bond on CBP Form 301, or its electronic equivalent in ACE eBond, containing the bond conditions set forth in 19 CFR 113.66, prior to release."],"Official answer: Entry or the payment of duty prior to release, subject to the provisions of the relevant CBP regulation. Look it up: 19 CFR 10.41a. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 35.)");
Q(9,3,2,"Of the four choices below, what is the CLASSIFICATION of a miniature billiards table designed for children from 8 to 12 years of age. It is composed of rosewood with cherry wood veneers and resin. The playing surface is a green synthetic mat.","9504.20.6000",["9403.30.8005", "9403.60.8081", "9503.00.0071"],"Official answer: 9504.20.6000 Look it up: 9504.20.6000, HTSUS; Note 1(l) to Chapter 94, HTSUS. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 36.)");
Q(9,3,2,"Of the four choices below, which is the best CLASSIFICATION of linoleum tiles? The tiles are composed of solidified linseed oil, pine resin, ground cork dust, sawdust, calcium carbonate (limestone powder), and have a felted synthetic fiber backing. The linoleum layer is two millimeters thick, the felted synthetic fiber backing is four millimeters thick, and the tiles are square in shape with each side measuring 24 centimeters in length.","5904.10.0000",["4504.10.3000", "5704.10.0090", "6806.00.0000"],"Official answer: 5904.10.0000 Look it up: 5904.10.0000, HTSUS; GRI 1. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 37.)");
Q(9,3,3,"Of the four choices below, which is the best CLASSIFICATION of sound insulating panels? The panels are two centimeters thick and imported in one by one half meter sections. They are composed of wood shavings agglomerated with a cement binder and are sanded but otherwise unfinished. To a casual observer, the panels look like a collection of randomly oriented wood slivers pressed together into a flat, rigid board. The intended use is for acoustic sound absorption for the construction of areas such as music studios.","6808.00.0000",["4411.14.1000", "4418.92.0000", "6806.90.0010"],"Official answer: 6808.00.0000 Look it up: 6808.00.0000, HTSUS; GRI 1; Chapter 44, Note 1(ij). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 38.)");
Q(9,3,2,"Of the four choices below, which is the best CLASSIFICATION for a plastic brush with metal bristles used to groom a dog or cat that at time of importation is valued at $3.64?","9603.90.8050",["9603.29.8010", "9603.29.8090", "9615.11.3000"],"Official answer: 9603.90.8050 Look it up: 9603.90.8050, HTSUS; GRI 1. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 39.)");
Q(9,3,2,"Of the four choices below, which is the best CLASSIFICATION of pure silver (99.99%) coins that are minted in South Africa, have legal tender status, and are valued at $25,000.00 U.S. Dollars (USD)?","7118.90.0055",["7106.91.1010", "7115.90.4000", "7118.10.0000"],"Official answer: 7118.90.0055 Look it up: 7118.90.0055, HTSUS; GRI 1; GRI 3(a). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 40.)");
Q(9,2,2,"Which of the following statements is TRUE about the procedure after detention of shipments under the Toxic Substances Control Act (TSCA) of a covered commodity?","The importer of the detained shipment must bring the shipment into compliance with Toxic Substances Control Act or export the shipment from the customs territory of the United States within 90 days after notice of detention or 30 days of demand for redelivery, whichever comes first.",["The importer of the detained shipment must submit written documentation to the Administrator with a copy to the port director within 90 days from the date of notice of detention, to show why the shipment should not be refused entry.", "The importer of the detained shipment must bring the shipment into compliance with Toxic Substances Control Act or export the shipment from the customs territory of the United States within 120 days after notice of detention or 60 days of demand for redelivery, whichever comes first.", "The importer of the detained shipment must submit written documentation to the Administrator with a copy to the port director within 120 days from the date of notice of detention, to show why the shipment should not be refused entry."],"Official answer: The importer of the detained shipment must bring the shipment into compliance with Toxic Substances Control Act or export the shipment from the customs territory of the United States within 90 days after notice of detention or 30 days of demand for redelivery, whichever comes first. Look it up: 19 CFR 12.124(a). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 41.)");
Q(9,2,2,"Which Federal agencies will notify CBP that a covered import does not comply with an applicable energy labeling standard?","Department of Energy (DOE) and Federal Trade Commission (FTC)",["U.S. Information Agency (USIA) and the U.S. Energy Information Administration (EIA)", "Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) and the Air Quality National Commission", "Department of Transportation (DOT) and the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration (NHTSA)"],"Official answer: Department of Energy (DOE) and Federal Trade Commission (FTC) Look it up: 19 CFR 12.50(a); 19 CFR 12.50(b). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 42.)");
Q(9,2,2,"When evaluating an application for Lever-rule protection against gray market articles in accordance with 19 CFR 133.2(e), which of the following is NOT a physical and material difference that CBP may consider between articles authorized for importation or sale in the United States and those not so authorized?","The retail price of the product authorized for sale in the United States and the retail price of the unauthorized gray market product.",["Differences resulting from legal or regulatory requirements, certification, etc.", "Differences in the formulation, product construction, structure, or composite product components, of both the authorized and gray market product.", "Performance and/or operational characteristics of both the authorized and gray product."],"Official answer: The retail price of the product authorized for sale in the United States and the retail price of the unauthorized gray market product. Look it up: 19 CFR 133.2(e). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 43.)");
Q(9,2,2,"Which of the following imported articles are subject to seizure pursuant to the Digital Millenium Copyright Act (17 USC 1201)? Assume all trademarks and copyrights referenced in the answers are recorded with CBP.","A memory card primarily designed to circumvent the copyright protection measures on the Nintendo Switch and enable the user to copy Nintendo’s copyrighted videogames for redistribution.",["Authentic DVDs for copyrighted Marvel movies that the importer manifested as blank CDs.", "A computer with a logo confusingly similar to a Dell trademark.", "An arcade machine with videogames substantially similar to Star Wars copyrights, and Disney has stated that the use of its copyrights is unauthorized."],"Official answer: A memory card primarily designed to circumvent the copyright protection measures on the Nintendo Switch and enable the user to copy Nintendo’s copyrighted videogames for redistribution. Look it up: 19 CFR 133.47; 19 CFR 133.22; 19 CFR 133.42. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 44.)");
Q(9,2,2,"A drawback claimant must furnish a properly executed drawback bond if approved for _____ privilege.","Accelerated payment",["Waiver of prior notice of intent to export", "Exporters summary procedure", "One time waiver of prior notice for past exports"],"Official answer: Accelerated payment Look it up: 19 CFR190.92(d). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 45.)");
Q(9,2,2,"What type of rejected merchandise is eligible for substitution drawback under 19 CFR 190.45?","Merchandise that was ultimately sold at retail and returned for any reason.",["Merchandise that did not conform to sample or specifications.", "Merchandise that was shipped without the consent of the consignee.", "Merchandise that was determined to be defective as of the time of importation."],"Official answer: Merchandise that was ultimately sold at retail and returned for any reason. Look it up: 19 CFR 190.45. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 46.)");
Q(9,2,2,"If an Activity Code 1- Basic Importation and Entry continuous bond will be transmitted to U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) pursuant to the Automated Commercial Environment (ACE) electronic bond system (eBond) test, using a CBP-approved Electronic Data Interchange (EDI), who must transmit it?","Surety or Surety Agent",["Port Director", "Importer", "Filer"],"Official answer: Surety or Surety Agent Look it up: ACE BRPD Chapter 3, Section 3.7. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 47.)");
Q(9,2,3,"HK Importers, Inc. (HKI) has a continuous basic importation and entry bond in the amount of $200,000.00 with Total Country Surety. HKI is a client of Best CHB (Best). Best has a continuous basic importation and entry bond with Thrift Surety in the amount of $200,000.00. Best filed entry on January 13, 2026, on behalf of HKI, with HKI listed as the importer of record, for a shipment of wooden chairs with an entered value of $40,000.00. The shipment was released on the same day. Best scheduled the duties, taxes and fees owed on the shipment for ACH payment to CBP nine business days after the date of entry. Best also prepared the entry summary information for transmission to CBP. Due to a computer error at Best, the entry summary information was not transmitted, nor was the payment made. CBP issued a liquidated damages demand in the amount of $100.00 for the failure to file the entry summary information. Which of the following statements is FALSE?","Best is liable to CBP for the payment of the liquidated damage demand amount because Best’s computer error caused the failure to file the entry summary data.",["HKI is liable for the payment of the liquidated damage demand amount because it is the importer of record.", "If the liquidated damage amount is not paid within 60 days of the demand and the principal has not filed a petition for relief, CBP will make a demand for payment on Total Country Surety.", "Thrift Surety has legal responsibility as a surety for the importation of the wooden chairs and may be notified by CBP if the duties owed are not tendered by HKI."],"Official answer: Best is liable to CBP for the payment of the liquidated damage demand amount because Best’s computer error caused the failure to file the entry summary data. Look it up: 19 CFR 113.62(a)(1)(i); 19 CFR 142.12(b); 19 CFR 141.101(a);. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 48.)");
Q(9,2,2,"Which entry type code indicates a re-warehouse entry?","Entry type code 22",["Entry type code 21", "Entry type code 31", "Entry type code 32"],"Official answer: Entry type code 22 Look it up: ACE BRPD Section 12.1. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 49.)");
Q(9,2,2,"Merchandise arrived at a port of entry and has been unladen from a vessel. No entry or entry summary has been filed. The merchandise can remain on the dock before it is deemed not timely entered for _____ days and the carrier has to give notice of unentered merchandise to a bonded warehouse certified by the port director as qualified to receive general order merchandise no later than _____ calendar days after landing.","15; 20",["20; 20", "30; 60", "10; 30"],"Official answer: 15; 20 Look it up: 19 CFR 141.5; 19 CFR 4.37(a); 19 CFR 4.37(c). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 50.)");
Q(9,2,2,"Absent the grant of an extension by the port director for reasonable cause, when must the operator of a Foreign Trade Zone or Subzone prepare their annual reconciliation report?","Within 90 days after the end of the zone/subzone year",["Within 30 days after the end of the zone/subzone year", "Within 45 days after the end of the zone/subzone year", "Within 120 days after the end of the zone/subzone year"],"Official answer: Within 90 days after the end of the zone/subzone year Look it up: 19 CFR 146.25(a). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 51.)");
Q(9,2,2,"To which of the following actions may merchandise admitted to a Foreign Trade Zone in zone-restricted status be subjected?","Exportation from the United States",["Transfer to customs territory for domestic consumption upon obtaining approval from a Center Director", "Modify zone status to domestic status", "Manipulation or manufacture into a distinct article of commerce"],"Official answer: Exportation from the United States Look it up: 19 CFR 146.44(a). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 52.)");
Q(9,2,2,"Which of the following best defines a domestic material?","A material whose country of origin is the same as the country in which the good is produced.",["A material used in the production, testing, or inspection of another good.", "A material that is interchangeable for commerical purposes and whose properties are essentially identical.", "A material that is grown or occurs organically rather than being processed or manufactured."],"Official answer: A material whose country of origin is the same as the country in which the good is produced. Look it up: 19 CFR 102.1(d). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 53.)");
Q(9,2,2,"Which of the following abbreviations or alternate spellings is unacceptable according to regulations found in 19 CFR 134?","Ita for Italy",["Luxemb for Luxembourg", "Gt. Britain for Great Britain", "Brasil for Brazil"],"Official answer: Ita for Italy Look it up: 19 CFR 134.45. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 54.)");
Q(9,2,2,"Unless exported or destroyed under Customs supervision prior to liquidation of the entry, articles without country of origin marking, as required, shall be subject to additional duties in the amount of:","10 percent of the final appraised value",["20 percent of the final appraised value", "50 percent of the final appraised value", "200 percent of the final appraised value"],"Official answer: 10 percent of the final appraised value Look it up: 19 CFR 134.2. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 55.)");
Q(9,2,2,"Under ACE Entry Summary Instructions, which of the following is TRUE regarding the reporting of gross shipping weight of goods?","Gross shipping weight must be reported in kilograms for all modes of transportation.",["Gross shipping weight is optional for goods transported by air.", "Gross shipping weight must include the container’s weight if shipped in lift vans.", "Gross shipping weight is optional for each line item in the entry summary."],"Official answer: Gross shipping weight must be reported in kilograms for all modes of transportation. Look it up: ACE Entry Summary Instructions, version 2.4a, page 18. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 56.)");
Q(9,2,2,"Of the following choices, when may the Surety Code \"999\" appropriately be entered on an entry summary?","When the bond is waived by regulation",["When a cash deposit is used in place of a bond", "When a single transaction bond amount is unknown", "When a continuous bond is not applicable"],"Official answer: When the bond is waived by regulation Look it up: ACE Entry Summary Instructions, version 2.4a, page 3. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 57.)");
Q(9,2,2,"Under which of the following circumstances would the Automated Commercial Environment (ACE) Cargo Release system generate a \"documents required\" message upon entry submission?","CBP and/or a partner government agency is requesting supporting documentation for further review.",["The merchandise is ready for release from Customs custody.", "The filer should correct or override the census warning and retransmit.", "CBP is issuing CBP Form 29, Notice of Action, to change the classification and/or value of the merchandise."],"Official answer: CBP and/or a partner government agency is requesting supporting documentation for further review. Look it up: ACE BRPD Section 1.2. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 58.)");
Q(9,2,2,"Under which circumstance may an individual who qualifies as an importer of record under 19 USC 1484 authorize an unpaid agent to enter merchandise on their behalf?","The individual, a person in the United States who is not a regular importer, is receiving a non- commercial shipment, and authorizes the unpaid agent in writing using power of attorney language.",["The individual, a known importer in the United States, is receiving in one shipment on one day merchandise that is a bona fide gift from a person in a foreign country and that has an aggregate fair retail value in the country of shipment of less than $100.00.", "The individual, a known importer in the United States not acting on behalf of a corporation, partnership, or association, makes a written request to the agent claiming to be indigent with proof that the individual cannot afford to pay the agent.", "The individual, a regular importer in the United States, appoints the agent to handle an importation of a single article of merchandise and the agent is a relative of the individual."],"Official answer: The individual, a person in the United States who is not a regular importer, is receiving a non- commercial shipment, and authorizes the unpaid agent in writing using power of attorney language. Look it up: Right to Make Entry Directive (CD 3530-002A). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 59.)");
Q(9,2,2,"In ACE, which of the following data elements CANNOT be changed through a Post Summary Correction (PSC) for any entry type?","Importer of Record",["Entry Type", "Harmonized Tariff Schedule classification", "Commercial Invoice Value"],"Official answer: Importer of Record Look it up: ACE BRPD Version 12.0, Section 6.5-6.6. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 60.)");
Q(9,2,2,"Which of the following is TRUE with respect to an underpayment or overpayment of duties, taxes, and fees determined upon reliquidation of an entry?","CBP must issue a refund for $7.56 in overpayment of duties, taxes, and fees, upon receiving a request from the importer of record in conjunction with a protest reliquidation.",["CBP may issue a bill for $14.63 in underpayment of duties, taxes, and fees, within 180 days of the original liquidation.", "CBP may issue a bill for underpayment or refund for overpayment of duties, taxes, and fees, irrespective of the sum entailed, upon receipt of a written request within 60 days of the original liquidation.", "CBP will refund a $15.42 overpayment on a reliquidated entry, reliquidated upon approval of a protest filed by the importer of record in which the importer indicated in the protest that it requested refunds of all amounts."],"Official answer: CBP must issue a refund for $7.56 in overpayment of duties, taxes, and fees, upon receiving a request from the importer of record in conjunction with a protest reliquidation. Look it up: and   19 CFR 173.3; 19 CFR 159.6. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 61.)");
Q(9,2,2,"Jane Doe is filing an entry summary. One line item is for a woman's dress, which is a woven garment of three different colors. In addition to the general information required on the accompanying commercial invoice, what other information that is specific to the dress must be provided on the invoice by regulation?","Whether there are two or more colors in the warp and/or filling in the dress fabric",["Whether the dress contains any embroidery, lace, braid, edging, trimming, piping or applique work", "Whether the dress has pockets sewn in", "The exact dimensions (length and width) of the dress"],"Official answer: Whether there are two or more colors in the warp and/or filling in the dress fabric Look it up: 19 CFR 141.89(a). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 62.)");
Q(9,2,3,"Johnson Consolidated Freight, Inc. (JCF) is the nominal consignee for a shipment of empty glass bottles, consigned to IPA (USA), Inc. (IPA), a beer brewer. JCF has an appropriate power of attorney with IPA. JCF contracts with Knowledgeable Customs Brokers, LLC (Knowledgeable) for Knowledgeable to file the entry and entry summary as the importer of record for the shipment. Knowledgeable obtains power of attorney from IPA and files the entry and entry summary with itself as the importer of record and obligates its basic importation and entry bond. Which one of the steps below is NOT one of the steps Knowledgeable will take to be relieved of liability for increased and additional duties owed under the facts above?","Knowledgeable will furnish within 90 days from the time of entry copies of the power of attorney documents between Knowledgeable and JCF and Knowledgeable and IPA proving that Knowledgeable had the right to make entry under 19 CFR 141.11.",["Knowledgeable will file a declaration within 90 days from the time of entry with CBP made by the actual owner of the merchandise acknowledging that the owner will pay all additional and increased duties along with a bond of the actual owner containing bond conditions set forth in 19 CFR 113.62.", "Knowledgeable will declare to CBP at the time of filing the entry and entry summary that it is not the actual owner of the goods.", "Knowledgeable will furnish to CBP at the time of filing the entry and entry summary the name and address of the actual owner of the goods."],"Official answer: Knowledgeable will furnish within 90 days from the time of entry copies of the power of attorney documents between Knowledgeable and JCF and Knowledgeable and IPA proving that Knowledgeable had the right to make entry under 19 CFR 141.11. Look it up: Right to Make Entry Directive (CD 3530-002A) Section 5.1.3;. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 63.)");
Q(9,2,3,"A customs broker timely and properly filed entry documentation (CBP Form 3461) for imported merchandise, without also filing an entry summary (CBP Form 7501), on September 15, 2025, and requested the time of entry to be on that same date. The merchandise, which was transported via vessel, had an estimated date of arrival of September 16, 2025. The merchandise arrived within the port limits on September 17, 2025. The merchandise was offloaded from the vessel on September 18, 2025. What is the time of entry?","September 17, 2025",["September 15, 2025", "September 16, 2025", "September 18, 2025"],"Official answer: September 17, 2025 Look it up: 19 CFR 141.68(a)(2); 19 CFR 141.68(e). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 64.)");
Q(9,2,2,"If a Center Director extends the liquidation of an entry, the official notice of extension and reasons for the extension will be posted on www.cbp.gov. What is the minimum timeframe that the notice of extension must be maintained on www.cbp.gov?","15 months",["1 month", "6 months", "12 months"],"Official answer: 15 months Look it up: 19 CFR 159.12(a)-(b). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 65.)");
Q(9,4,2,"As part of an investigation, CBP has decided that there is reasonable suspicion that an importer entered covered merchandise into the U.S. through evasion of anti-dumping duties under the Enforce and Protect Act of 2015 (EAPA). The entries are unliquidated. What interim measure must CBP take with respect to the entries?","Suspend the liquidation of each unliquidated entry of such covered merchandise that entered on or after the date of the initiation of the investigation under 19 CFR 165.15.",["Schedule oral discussions to occur with the importer within 200 calendar days to continue the investigation.", "Provide a public version of the administrative record of this investigation online within 15 business days after notifying the U.S. Department of Commerce.", "Extend the period for liquidating each unliquidated entry of such covered merchandise that entered after the date the investigation was initiated."],"Official answer: Suspend the liquidation of each unliquidated entry of such covered merchandise that entered on or after the date of the initiation of the investigation under 19 CFR 165.15. Look it up: 19 CFR 165.24(b)(1)(i)-(iii); 19 CFR 165.24(c). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 66.)");
Q(9,4,2,"Which of the following is TRUE with respect to imported merchandise after the U.S. International Trade Commission (ITC) finds a violation of section 337 of the Tariff Act of 1930 (19 USC 1337) and issues an exclusion order, as implemented by CBP under 19 CFR 12.39?","Merchandise subject to the exclusion order may be entered under bond until the determination of a violation becomes final.",["The exclusion order is not effective until 60 days after issuance, at which point merchandise subject to the exclusion order no longer may be entered.", "Merchandise subject to the exclusion order may not be entered under any circumstance after the exclusion order has been issued.", "Merchandise subject to the exclusion order may be entered under a basic importation and entry bond in an amount set by CBP."],"Official answer: Merchandise subject to the exclusion order may be entered under bond until the determination of a violation becomes final. Look it up: 19 CFR 12.39, 19 CFR 113. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 67.)");
Q(9,4,2,"Coffee Inc., a Canadian company, buys raw coffee beans from Colombia classified under the Harmonized Tariff Schedule (HTS) subheading 0901.11. Coffee Inc. then processes the beans into instant coffee classified under 2101.11.21. The instant coffee is then imported into the U.S. for sale. In order for the instant coffee to qualify for preferential tariff treatment under the United States-Mexico- Canada Agreement (USMCA), which of the following statements must be TRUE?","The nonoriginating coffee must constitute no more than 60 percent by weight of the good.",["The nonoriginating coffee must constitute no more than 75 percent by weight of the good.", "The nonoriginating coffee must constitute no less than 60 percent by weight of the good.", "The nonoriginating coffee must have a Labor Value Content (LVC) of thirty percent, with at least 15 percentage points of high-wage material."],"Official answer: The nonoriginating coffee must constitute no more than 60 percent by weight of the good. Look it up: General Note 11(o), Chapter 21, HTSUS. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 68.)");
Q(9,4,2,"Which of the following Special Program Indicators (SPI) is NOT valid?","BU",["S", "S+", "P"],"Official answer: BU Look it up: HTSUS General Note 3(c)(i). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 69.)");
Q(9,4,2,"Which of the following is considered an assist and should be included in the entered value?","The cost of printed circuit assembly components for switch integration that are supplied free of charge by a U.S. importer to a foreign producer of switches in Taipei, Taiwan.",["The cost of engineering plans produced in Omaha, Nebraska for switch assemblies that are supplied free of charge by a U.S. importer to a foreign producer of switches in Taipei, Taiwan.", "The cost of printed circuit assemblies that will be integrated into timing switches that are purchased by a foreign producer of switch assemblies produced in Taipei, Taiwan.", "The cost of a factory air conditioning system that was supplied free of charge by a U.S. importer to a foreign producer of switches in Taipei, Taiwan."],"Official answer: The cost of printed circuit assembly components for switch integration that are supplied free of charge by a U.S. importer to a foreign producer of switches in Taipei, Taiwan. Look it up: 19 CFR 152.102(a). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 70.)");
Q(9,4,2,"Of the following importation scenarios, which has a Transaction Value?","A foreign manufacturer sells merchandise to a related U.S. importer. The foreign seller does not sell identical merchandise or similar merchandise to any unrelated parties. The transaction between the foreign seller and the U.S. importer is determined by Customs to be unaffected by the relationship.",["While negotiating business and transaction terms with an intended importer, the foreign manufacturer imports 10,000 refrigerator time switches to a storage facility in an effort to beat pending tariff increases.", "An event operator is hosting a large public aerospace convention and is importing multiple goods from multiple manufacturers for consignment sale. The articles are not samples and will be sold to convention attendees.", "A foreign manufacturer of industrial drones is importing promotional steel airplane models to be given to each purchaser of a drone as a thank you gift."],"Official answer: A foreign manufacturer sells merchandise to a related U.S. importer. The foreign seller does not sell identical merchandise or similar merchandise to any unrelated parties. The transaction between the foreign seller and the U.S. importer is determined by Customs to be unaffected by the relationship. Look it up: 19 CFR 152.103(j)(2)(i). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 71.)");
Q(9,4,2,"A shipment of plastic optical fibers manufactured in China is valued at $95,678.00 and imported under HTS 9001.10.0050. What is the total applicable duty amount for this shipment? Note: duties based upon Executive Orders which are not part of the examination references are not inlcuded.","$30,329.93",["$6,410.43", "$23,919.50", "$15,978.23"],"Official answer: $30,329.93 Look it up: 9001.10.0050, HTSUS; 9903.88.02, HTSUS. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 72.)");
Q(9,4,3,"A&G, a Minneapolis furniture company, has agreed to purchase 5000 bespoke ironing boards from Kraftsmen, a furniture manufacturer in Slovenia. A&G supplies Kraftsmen with the following inputs to use in the production of the ironing boards, free of charge: • Design specifications created by a Dallas-based furniture designer, purchased for a flat fee of $3200.00. • 2500 yards of cotton fabric, which Kraftsmen procured for $2.00 per yard in Slovenia, then shipped to A&G’s screen printer in Mozambique. A&G paid their screen printer to add a design to the fabric for $1.00 per yard, before returning the fabric to Kraftsmen’s factory in Slovenia. A&G imports the ironing boards to the United States in five shipments of 1000 ironing boards each. A&G agrees to pay Kraftsmen $375,000.00 for the entire order of 5000 ironing boards, not including the value of the assists. If A&G chooses for the total assist value to be apportioned over the entire anticipated production of 5000 ironing boards, what is the dutiable value of one shipment of 1000 ironing boards?","$75,500.00",["$76,000.00", "$76,140.00", "$77,140.00"],"Official answer: $75,500.00 Look it up: 19 CFR 151.102-103. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 74.)");
Q(9,4,2,"A foreign subsidiary sells ore to its U.S. parent company. The price of the ore charged by the subsidiary to its parent is the price of ore on the New York Mercantile Exchange on the date the ore arrives in the United States. Select the statement that is TRUE regarding this price.","The price is acceptable under transaction value as it is based upon a formula which neither party can control.",["The price is not acceptable because it is not known at the time of shipment.", "The price is not acceptable because the parties are related.", "The price is acceptable under deductive value because it is based on the price of ore sold in the United States."],"Official answer: The price is acceptable under transaction value as it is based upon a formula which neither party can control. Look it up: 19 CFR 152.103(a)(1). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 75.)");
Q(9,4,3,"A U.S. buyer purchased merchandise from an unrelated manufacturer in India. The terms of the sale were ex-factory packed. The U.S. buyer paid the foreign manufacturer $18,344 which included a $1,500 packing fee. The U.S. buyer also paid $4,500 to a freight forwarder to cover expenses for inland freight of $500 and air freight of $4,000. In addition, the U.S. buyer paid a commission of $1,500 to a buying agent who facilitated the purchase of the merchandise. What is the entered value for the imported merchandise?","$18,344.00",["$12,344.00", "$6,500.00", "$25,844.00"],"Official answer: $18,344.00 Look it up: 19 CFR 152.103(a)(5)(i). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 76.)");
Q(9,4,2,"Footwear incorporating a protective metal toe-cap imported on or after July 1, 1981 will be determined in accordance with _____.","Section 402, Tariff Act of 1930 (19 USC 1401a), as amended by section 201, Trade Agreements Act of 1979.",["Section 402, Tariff Act of 1930 (19 USC 1401a)", "Statement of Administrative Action relating to customs valuation, submitted to and approved by Congress along with the Trade Agreements Act of 1979 (Pub. L. 96-39)", "T.D. 81-7, 46 FR 2600, January 12, 1981, as amended by T.D. 89-1, 53 DR 51270, Dec. 21, 1988"],"Official answer: Section 402, Tariff Act of 1930 (19 USC 1401a), as amended by section 201, Trade Agreements Act of 1979. Look it up: 6401.10.00, HTSUS; 19 CFR 152.101(a). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 77.)");
Q(9,4,2,"Luxury Resins, a Japanese producer of plastic materials, sold resin to Phone Cases Plus, an unrelated U.S. manufacturer, for $20,000. Later, Phone Cases Plus decides to pay a $2,000 royalty to Disney for the right to manufacture and sell Disney-branded phone cases. It makes these phone cases out of the resin purchased from Luxury Resins. What is the transaction value of the transaction between Luxury Resins and Phone Cases Plus?","$20,000.00",["$18,000.00", "$22,000.00", "$2,000.00"],"Official answer: $20,000.00 Look it up: 19 CFR 152.103(f). (CBP April 2026 exam, question 78.)");
Q(9,4,2,"If the commercial invoice is in a foreign currency, the foreign currency shall be converted to U.S. dollars using the proclaimed rate or certified rate or rates effective on the _____.","Date of export",["Date of import", "Date of entry", "Date of lading"],"Official answer: Date of export Look it up: 19 CFR 159.32. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 79.)");
Q(9,4,2,"During a cargo examination of entered goods, a CBP officer discovered that none of the physical merchandise agrees with the invoice submitted with the entry. The CBP officer determines that there was no evidence of any intent to defraud. The original entry must be replaced by a new entry. How will the estimated duties paid on the original entry be treated?","The estimated duties paid on the original entry will be refunded on liquidation as in the case of a nonimportation.",["The estimated duties paid on the original entry will be transferred to the new entry via ACH transfer.", "The estimated duties paid on the original entry will be applied to the importer’s next periodic monthly statement as a credit against the amount due on the statement.", "The estimated duties paid on the original entry will be retained by CBP and applied against the forthcoming forfeiture proceedings."],"Official answer: The estimated duties paid on the original entry will be refunded on liquidation as in the case of a nonimportation. Look it up: 19 CFR 152.3. (CBP April 2026 exam, question 80.)");
